WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4509.

2
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.400
Begin Session III.]

3
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.400
<v HAKI VINCENT>So I got prostate cancer.

4
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.200
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Oh, no.

5
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.000
<v VINCENT>Behind that.

6
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm sorry to hear that.

7
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:08.400
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

8
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.200
That's the way the government do us.

9
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.000
Use us up throw us away.

10
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.921
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you want to get that on tape?

11
00:00:16.921 --> 00:00:20.206
<v VINCENT>Yeah, I'll put it on tape it's no problem.

12
00:00:20.206 --> 00:00:20.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

13
00:00:20.571 --> 00:00:20.937
So . . .

14
00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:22.032
<v KYLE TANGLAO>Whenever you're ready.

15
00:00:22.032 --> 00:00:23.857
<v ARSENEAULT>Today is August twenty-seventh, 2015.

16
00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:31.524
This is Chelsea Arseneault with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History and I'm here again with Mr. Haki Vincent.

17
00:00:31.524 --> 00:00:36.270
This is our second session together at his home in Mossville where I . . .

18
00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.000
<v VINCENT>Third.

19
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:37.372
<v ARSENEAULT>Third.

20
00:00:37.372 --> 00:00:39.605
It is our third session together.

21
00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:42.209
But our second one at your house.

22
00:00:42.209 --> 00:00:43.326
Yes, you're right.

23
00:00:43.326 --> 00:00:43.698
<v VINCENT>Yes.

24
00:00:43.698 --> 00:00:45.186
<v ARSENEAULT>We've spoken three times.

25
00:00:45.186 --> 00:00:49.279
He is currently right now . . . What are you doing Mr. Haki?

26
00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:51.512
What are you doing right now?

27
00:00:51.512 --> 00:00:54.116
<v VINCENT>I'm making some signs for fresh produce.

28
00:00:54.116 --> 00:00:54.860
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

29
00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:55.605
Got you.

30
00:00:55.605 --> 00:00:56.349
He's got . . .

31
00:00:56.349 --> 00:00:58.209
<v VINCENT>That's growing as we speak.

32
00:00:58.209 --> 00:00:58.581
<v ARSENEAULT>Nice.

33
00:00:58.581 --> 00:01:06.023
And we spoke about a lot last week but you were just telling us about going to the VA hospital.

34
00:01:06.023 --> 00:01:09.000
Do you want to share that again?

35
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:09.696
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

36
00:01:09.696 --> 00:01:25.714
I went to the VA hospital last week and it was explained to me that in nineteen . . . Between 1953 . . . Is that too loud?

37
00:01:25.714 --> 00:01:28.500
<v TANGLAO>Could I lower it?

38
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.679
<v VINCENT>Yeah, you can cut it off.

39
00:01:32.679 --> 00:01:36.161
Just turn the key off.

40
00:01:36.161 --> 00:01:38.946
<v ARSENEAULT>Be a nice background.

41
00:01:38.946 --> 00:01:41.732
<v TANGLAO>Turn the key off?

42
00:01:41.732 --> 00:01:42.429
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

43
00:01:42.429 --> 00:01:45.214
I was . . . Still on?

44
00:01:45.214 --> 00:01:45.911
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

45
00:01:45.911 --> 00:01:48.000
You're good.

46
00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:02.327
<v VINCENT>I was exposed to Agent Orange between 1953 is when I went in and 1955 I was exposed to Agent Orange.

47
00:02:02.327 --> 00:02:07.785
And as a result I have prostate cancer.

48
00:02:07.785 --> 00:02:11.196
Now the VA don't care.

49
00:02:11.196 --> 00:02:15.972
They demonstrate that by not doing anything.

50
00:02:15.972 --> 00:02:18.701
<v ARSENEAULT>What is Agent Orange?

51
00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:21.430
Just for the record.

52
00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:30.299
<v VINCENT>Agent Orange is a chemical that they use to kill vegetation . . . and people.

53
00:02:30.299 --> 00:02:36.439
<v ARSENEAULT>You were exposed to it when you were overseas?

54
00:02:36.439 --> 00:02:37.121
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

55
00:02:37.121 --> 00:02:57.589
Well, when I was in the military because we were . . . I was in the air force and they carried anything they want to carry on the planes with the people.

56
00:02:57.589 --> 00:03:01.000
So that's the four-one-one.

57
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:01.974
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

58
00:03:01.974 --> 00:03:08.789
Where you exposed to it actually . . . Were they spraying it while you were there?

59
00:03:08.789 --> 00:03:12.684
<v VINCENT>No. Well, I don't know whether they were.

60
00:03:12.684 --> 00:03:16.579
It was probably on the plane with us.

61
00:03:16.579 --> 00:03:17.066
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

62
00:03:17.066 --> 00:03:20.961
<v VINCENT>You know, because they carried bombs and everything.

63
00:03:20.961 --> 00:03:25.342
You know, dealing with warfare so that's the four-one-one.

64
00:03:25.342 --> 00:03:25.829
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

65
00:03:25.829 --> 00:03:35.079
Well we did want to talk about your activism, because I don't think we've gotten a chance to really . . .

66
00:03:35.079 --> 00:03:36.539
<v VINCENT>Talk about what?

67
00:03:36.539 --> 00:03:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Your activism.

68
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.200
<v VINCENT>[laughs] You [reading?].

69
00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:39.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

70
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.400
Could we talk about some of these?

71
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:42.800
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

72
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:43.600
Let me . . .

73
00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

74
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.600
Do you need to?

75
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.600
<v TANGLAO>You want to pause it?

76
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:54.800
<v ARSENEAULT>We wanted to talk about your activism because we know that was a big part of your life.

77
00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.000
And I wanted to ask you when you first became politically aware?

78
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.297
<v VINCENT>When they attempted to draft me.

79
00:04:04.297 --> 00:04:16.473
So I didn't want to go into the army so I went into the air force instead.

80
00:04:16.473 --> 00:04:20.054
Which was no real difference.

81
00:04:20.054 --> 00:04:25.068
All of it's hazardous to your health.

82
00:04:25.068 --> 00:04:27.932
<v ARSENEAULT>What age was that?

83
00:04:27.932 --> 00:04:38.676
<v VINCENT>I put my age up to seventeen to go in and get it over with.

84
00:04:38.676 --> 00:04:42.973
<v ARSENEAULT>When would you say you were . . .?

85
00:04:42.973 --> 00:04:47.986
<v VINCENT>I was really sixteen at the time.

86
00:04:47.986 --> 00:04:48.703
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

87
00:04:48.703 --> 00:04:53.000
They allowed you to enlist?

88
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.667
<v VINCENT>Well I put my age up so yeah.

89
00:04:57.667 --> 00:05:00.583
<v ARSENEAULT>You just forged the papers?

90
00:05:00.583 --> 00:05:05.250
<v VINCENT>[Laughs] Well you know they didn't care anyway.

91
00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:05.833
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

92
00:05:05.833 --> 00:05:09.333
<v VINCENT>Because they wanted bodies you know.

93
00:05:09.333 --> 00:05:19.250
You could have been blind, crippled, and crazy, they would have took you at that time [laughs].

94
00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:19.833
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

95
00:05:19.833 --> 00:05:26.833
So when would you say you became, you first became, politically active?

96
00:05:26.833 --> 00:05:28.000
<v VINCENT>Then.

97
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.642
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

98
00:05:29.642 --> 00:05:37.030
<v VINCENT>When I was forced to go in the military.

99
00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:44.418
I was a mad camper so I started getting

100
00:05:44.418 --> 00:05:46.060
<c.p>active then.

101
00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:50.164
<v ARSENEAULT>In what kind of ways?

102
00:05:50.164 --> 00:05:55.910
<v VINCENT>By not really participating in the war.

103
00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:59.194
I was not cooperating.

104
00:05:59.194 --> 00:06:11.507
I make sure a lot of stuff didn't work that was supposed to be working.

105
00:06:11.507 --> 00:06:16.433
I just leave it like that.

106
00:06:16.433 --> 00:06:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Anything else you remember about that time?

107
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:30.867
<v VINCENT>I came from Chocolate City, Washington, D.C.

108
00:06:30.867 --> 00:06:42.106
They don't call it Chocolate City anymore because everything changed.

109
00:06:42.106 --> 00:06:55.593
They were moving the blacks out and the whites in Washington, D.C.

110
00:06:55.593 --> 00:07:03.460
<v ARSENEAULT>What neighborhood did you grow up in?

111
00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:05.708
<v VINCENT>Far northeast.

112
00:07:05.708 --> 00:07:24.814
Which is a historical area because the people who came there were, you know, ex-slaves and whatnot.

113
00:07:24.814 --> 00:07:30.434
So we actually built Deanwood.

114
00:07:30.434 --> 00:07:40.549
That's what it was called, Deanwood. [ . . .?] [phone rings] Haki.

115
00:07:40.549 --> 00:07:52.912
Yes. [various phone noises] I’m not familiar with all this stuff.

116
00:07:52.912 --> 00:07:57.407
I think that's what . . .

117
00:07:57.407 --> 00:08:04.150
<v ARSENEAULT>Are you trying to hang up?

118
00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:09.770
<v VINCENT>That's Washington calling me now.

119
00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:10.894
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

120
00:08:10.894 --> 00:08:13.142
Deanwood's calling?

121
00:08:13.142 --> 00:08:16.513
<v VINCENT>Let me see.

122
00:08:16.513 --> 00:08:23.257
Whoever it is is busy now.

123
00:08:23.257 --> 00:08:30.000
What was your question again?

124
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.758
<v ARSENEAULT>What you remember about your neighborhood.

125
00:08:33.758 --> 00:08:38.768
What you remember about your neighborhood in Washington.

126
00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:41.274
That was the question.

127
00:08:41.274 --> 00:08:43.153
<v VINCENT>It was country.

128
00:08:43.153 --> 00:08:47.537
People had chickens, pigs, stuff like that.

129
00:08:47.537 --> 00:08:53.800
Had gardens and plenty of open space to enjoy nature.

130
00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:58.184
Back in those days we had outhouses.

131
00:08:58.184 --> 00:09:00.689
Most people had outhouses.

132
00:09:00.689 --> 00:09:11.963
We were a little better off than most people in that area because my father was a builder.

133
00:09:11.963 --> 00:09:17.600
So we had inside bathrooms and stuff like that.

134
00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:19.479
Everybody was friendly.

135
00:09:19.479 --> 00:09:26.995
There wasn't a whole lot of chaos, you know, everybody was neighbors.

136
00:09:26.995 --> 00:09:30.126
So we got along good.

137
00:09:30.126 --> 00:09:33.258
Wasn’t no chaos going on.

138
00:09:33.258 --> 00:09:42.026
And every now and then they would have what they call an air raid.

139
00:09:42.026 --> 00:09:44.532
What they call it?

140
00:09:44.532 --> 00:09:46.411
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a drill?

141
00:09:46.411 --> 00:09:47.037
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

142
00:09:47.037 --> 00:09:50.795
Like they would ring the siren.

143
00:09:50.795 --> 00:10:04.574
Everybody had to go in at night, turn off their lights, and they had wardens walking around making sure everybody was inside.

144
00:10:04.574 --> 00:10:08.958
You know, all the lights were out.

145
00:10:08.958 --> 00:10:19.605
So if any aircraft come over they couldn't see, you know, where the city was and whatnot.

146
00:10:19.605 --> 00:10:25.242
So that was a kind of traumatic time then.

147
00:10:25.242 --> 00:10:25.868
But . . .

148
00:10:25.868 --> 00:10:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I can only imagine.

149
00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:29.606
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

150
00:10:29.606 --> 00:10:36.879
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were your . . . Do you remember anything about your neighbors in Deanwood?

151
00:10:36.879 --> 00:10:40.515
Who were your neighbors growing up?

152
00:10:40.515 --> 00:10:43.545
<v VINCENT>You said who were they?

153
00:10:43.545 --> 00:10:44.152
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

154
00:10:44.152 --> 00:10:45.364
<v VINCENT>Name-wise? [Laughs]

155
00:10:45.364 --> 00:10:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Anything you remember about them.

156
00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:55.479
<v VINCENT>Well the guy next door to us . . . Who were their names . . . Henson.

157
00:10:55.479 --> 00:10:59.798
They didn't have running water in their house.

158
00:10:59.798 --> 00:11:05.737
And one thing I remember, we used to sell them water.

159
00:11:05.737 --> 00:11:11.136
Or they used to pay us for buckets of water.

160
00:11:11.136 --> 00:11:19.235
So each one of us be trying to be the first one home after school.

161
00:11:19.235 --> 00:11:21.394
<v ARSENEAULT>To get the money?

162
00:11:21.394 --> 00:11:21.934
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

163
00:11:21.934 --> 00:11:25.174
To take the water to them.

164
00:11:25.174 --> 00:11:32.192
To get the twenty-five cents or whatever it was for water, you know.

165
00:11:32.192 --> 00:11:40.291
And we had . . . We lived on Hayes Street, and the next block was Fiftieth Street.

166
00:11:40.291 --> 00:11:44.070
And it was a little club there.

167
00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:50.009
I remember one main thing about it Joe Louis was fighting.

168
00:11:50.009 --> 00:11:50.549
I

169
00:11:50.549 --> 00:11:58.108
<c.p>don't remember who he was fighting, but people didn't have . . . Everybody didn't have TVs.

170
00:11:58.108 --> 00:12:05.667
Only a few people around there had TVs and they were black and white.

171
00:12:05.667 --> 00:12:21.324
So this club or tavern had a TV in it and when Joe Louis was getting ready to fight the people were all around trying to see the TV.

172
00:12:21.324 --> 00:12:23.484
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he a boxer?

173
00:12:23.484 --> 00:12:24.023
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

174
00:12:24.023 --> 00:12:27.263
He was something like Muhammad Ali.

175
00:12:27.263 --> 00:12:36.441
Yeah, so people were hanging on the screen doors and everything trying to look inside the TV.

176
00:12:36.441 --> 00:12:40.761
So that's one thing I remember real well.

177
00:12:40.761 --> 00:12:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all have a TV?

178
00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.480
<v VINCENT>No, we didn't have a TV.

179
00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:52.680
Matter of fact, the neighbor that we used to take water he had a TV.

180
00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:56.813
I don't know if y'all have seen it or not.

181
00:12:56.813 --> 00:12:59.707
The TV screen was about that big.

182
00:12:59.707 --> 00:13:03.013
<v ARSENEAULT>And the rest of the set was huge?

183
00:13:03.013 --> 00:13:03.427
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

184
00:13:03.427 --> 00:13:05.493
<v ARSENEAULT>A bunch of wooden stuff?

185
00:13:05.493 --> 00:13:13.347
<v VINCENT>Yeah, and they had a big magnifying glass in front of the TV to make it look bigger. [laughs]

186
00:13:13.347 --> 00:13:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's really smart.

187
00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:15.530
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

188
00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:20.301
So that's one thing I really remember about TV.

189
00:13:20.301 --> 00:13:24.012
A little later on we got one.

190
00:13:24.012 --> 00:13:27.193
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all have a favorite show?

191
00:13:27.193 --> 00:13:28.783
<v VINCENT>Let me see.

192
00:13:28.783 --> 00:13:35.675
Howdy Doody was a, you know, children's show with little clowns and whatnot.

193
00:13:35.675 --> 00:13:40.446
But I didn't . . . I wasn't into TV too much.

194
00:13:40.446 --> 00:13:46.807
I was mostly outside or helping my father do stuff, you know.

195
00:13:46.807 --> 00:13:55.819
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm just curious, what would y'all do with the twenty-five cents that you got for the water?

196
00:13:55.819 --> 00:13:56.349
<v VINCENT>Sweets.

197
00:13:56.349 --> 00:13:57.410
<v ARSENEAULT>Like candy?

198
00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:57.940
<v VINCENT>Treats.

199
00:13:57.940 --> 00:13:59.000
Sure.

200
00:13:59.000 --> 00:13:59.884
<v ARSENEAULT>From where?

201
00:13:59.884 --> 00:14:08.282
<v VINCENT>There was a store not far from us in the next block or so, was a store close by.

202
00:14:08.282 --> 00:14:19.773
So we'd go to the store and we had a movie called Strand Theater close by so we would save it and go to the movies.

203
00:14:19.773 --> 00:14:23.751
Movies probably just cost twenty-five cents to get in.

204
00:14:23.751 --> 00:14:27.287
<v ARSENEAULT>It was twenty-five cents to see a movie?

205
00:14:27.287 --> 00:14:30.823
<v VINCENT>About twenty-five cents to go to the movie.

206
00:14:30.823 --> 00:14:40.989
On Saturdays if we were good and did what we were supposed to do we get a chance to go to the movie.

207
00:14:40.989 --> 00:14:45.409
And we had a street car used to come through.

208
00:14:45.409 --> 00:14:48.061
They call it a trolley car.

209
00:14:48.061 --> 00:14:58.227
But it came from downtown Washington and it went to the Maryland line turned around, you know, that's the way it was run.

210
00:14:58.227 --> 00:15:10.160
So we had . . . I think I told y'all before we had built an amusement park because the amusement park in Virginia that people go to was racist,

211
00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:15.464
prejudice, whatever you want to call it and we couldn't go there.

212
00:15:15.464 --> 00:15:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It was just . . . It was segregated or . . .?

213
00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:19.515
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

214
00:15:19.515 --> 00:15:26.727
So we built one in our community and then most people would come there.

215
00:15:26.727 --> 00:15:33.939
They would get to ride the street car to the . . . to our amusement park.

216
00:15:33.939 --> 00:15:34.455
Yeah.

217
00:15:34.455 --> 00:15:39.091
It was right across the street from us so . . .

218
00:15:39.091 --> 00:15:41.152
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it still there?

219
00:15:41.152 --> 00:15:41.667
<v VINCENT>[Pardon]?

220
00:15:41.667 --> 00:15:43.727
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it still there?

221
00:15:43.727 --> 00:15:45.788
<v VINCENT>No, it’s been gone.

222
00:15:45.788 --> 00:15:49.394
But I remember one night we would . . .

223
00:15:49.394 --> 00:15:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Those trucks keep going, don't they?

224
00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:53.553
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

225
00:15:53.553 --> 00:15:54.659
All day.

226
00:15:54.659 --> 00:15:55.764
All night.

227
00:15:55.764 --> 00:16:00.740
But we didn't lock doors and stuff, you know.

228
00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:09.033
Everybody was friends and whatnot so wasn't no a whole lot of locking stuff up.

229
00:16:09.033 --> 00:16:19.537
But one night a drunk man came in the house, laid down on the couch, and went to sleep.

230
00:16:19.537 --> 00:16:30.041
And my mother woke up and saw him and, "What you doing up in here?" [Laughs] Put him out.

231
00:16:30.041 --> 00:16:30.593
But . . .

232
00:16:30.593 --> 00:16:35.016
<v ARSENEAULT>He probably didn't even know where he was.

233
00:16:35.016 --> 00:16:41.650
<v VINCENT>No. Probably went to the amusement park and got drunk, you know.

234
00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:42.203
Yeah.

235
00:16:42.203 --> 00:16:43.309
<v ARSENEAULT>That's funny.

236
00:16:43.309 --> 00:16:43.862
Wow.

237
00:16:43.862 --> 00:16:48.285
<v VINCENT>So it was good time coming up then.

238
00:16:48.285 --> 00:16:48.837
Yeah.

239
00:16:48.837 --> 00:16:54.919
<v ARSENEAULT>What . . . I think I've asked you this before but I forget.

240
00:16:54.919 --> 00:17:01.000
What would your parents tell you about Mossville growing up?

241
00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:14.080
<v VINCENT>They would tell us that we had a lot of land in Mossville from our grandfather who was Eli Vincent, Senior.

242
00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:20.309
And we need to go and experience it, you know.

243
00:17:20.309 --> 00:17:24.046
So that's how I got here.

244
00:17:24.046 --> 00:17:40.863
They brought me here when I was maybe about six months old and my mother was telling me about I was a big eater and drinker then.

245
00:17:40.863 --> 00:17:43.977
A lot of milk and . . .

246
00:17:43.977 --> 00:17:47.714
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were a fat baby?

247
00:17:47.714 --> 00:17:48.337
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

248
00:17:48.337 --> 00:18:01.417
And my . . . I was crying one day or whatever and my grandfather said, "Wait a minute, what's wrong with that boy?"

249
00:18:01.417 --> 00:18:04.531
"Oh, he wants some milk."

250
00:18:04.531 --> 00:18:10.137
So he brought a cow up by the house.

251
00:18:10.137 --> 00:18:11.383
Milk cow.

252
00:18:11.383 --> 00:18:16.366
Tied him up there so I got fatter.

253
00:18:16.366 --> 00:18:18.857
Plenty of cow milk.

254
00:18:18.857 --> 00:18:20.726
<v ARSENEAULT>That'll do it.

255
00:18:20.726 --> 00:18:26.331
<v VINCENT>Yeah, and he was a farmer and a carpenter.

256
00:18:26.331 --> 00:18:33.806
So he would share a lot of his vegetables with the community.

257
00:18:33.806 --> 00:18:35.051
Mossville community.

258
00:18:35.051 --> 00:18:45.640
People come, you know, didn't have no food and whatnot he would share the food with them.

259
00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:50.000
Because he was pretty well off.

260
00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:51.273
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Eli was?

261
00:18:51.273 --> 00:18:51.697
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

262
00:18:51.697 --> 00:18:53.394
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he live here?

263
00:18:53.394 --> 00:18:53.818
<v VINCENT>Yes.

264
00:18:53.818 --> 00:18:55.939
<v ARSENEAULT>In one of these houses?

265
00:18:55.939 --> 00:18:57.636
<v VINCENT>Oh no, not here.

266
00:18:57.636 --> 00:19:02.727
He lived up on the bigger piece of land that we had.

267
00:19:02.727 --> 00:19:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

268
00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:10.216
<v VINCENT>Matter of fact they just start clearing it off.

269
00:19:10.216 --> 00:19:19.887
They actually stole it through the methods they use to take things from people.

270
00:19:19.887 --> 00:19:21.959
<v ARSENEAULT>Who stole it?

271
00:19:21.959 --> 00:19:30.247
<v VINCENT>Between the government and the white neighbor called Krause &amp; Managan Lumber Company.

272
00:19:30.247 --> 00:19:42.680
So after they stole it they sold it to Sasol [oil refinery] who cleared it not long ago.

273
00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:45.443
<v ARSENEAULT>Krause &amp; Managan you say?

274
00:19:45.443 --> 00:19:46.134
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

275
00:19:46.134 --> 00:19:48.897
Krause &amp; Managan Lumber Company.

276
00:19:48.897 --> 00:19:58.567
But they were trying to get it from him way back in the day.

277
00:19:58.567 --> 00:20:02.021
But he wouldn't sell it.

278
00:20:02.021 --> 00:20:06.165
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they want to clear it?

279
00:20:06.165 --> 00:20:11.000
Why did they want the land?

280
00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:21.111
<v VINCENT>I guess they wanted more land and he had . . . It was rich land.

281
00:20:21.111 --> 00:20:36.667
He had timber on it and clear land where he used to raise all his vegetables and stuff like that.

282
00:20:36.667 --> 00:20:41.333
So that's what happened to that.

283
00:20:41.333 --> 00:20:49.111
So they had been trying to get it for years.

284
00:20:49.111 --> 00:20:58.444
But they finally did what they had to do to get it.

285
00:20:58.444 --> 00:21:03.111
<v ARSENEAULT>Who does it belong to now?

286
00:21:03.111 --> 00:21:18.667
<v VINCENT>It still belong to us but it's a matter of a lot of litigation involved to reclaim it, you know.

287
00:21:18.667 --> 00:21:24.111
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you think it'll all work out?

288
00:21:24.111 --> 00:21:38.889
<v VINCENT>It depends on the youngsters in the family and whether they do what they need to do, you know.

289
00:21:38.889 --> 00:21:43.556
So it's really hard to say.

290
00:21:43.556 --> 00:21:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Such a nice day for painting.

291
00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:49.814
<v VINCENT>Yes.

292
00:21:49.814 --> 00:21:56.330
It's a nice day to be outside, period.

293
00:21:56.330 --> 00:22:10.990
<v ARSENEAULT>So did your parents ever tell you any stories about Mossville while you were growing up in D.C.?

294
00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:11.804
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

295
00:22:11.804 --> 00:22:21.577
My father and my uncle used to tell me stories about, yeah.

296
00:22:21.577 --> 00:22:28.907
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they tell you about like growing up here?

297
00:22:28.907 --> 00:22:34.608
Or did they both grow up here?

298
00:22:34.608 --> 00:22:38.680
Your father and your uncle?

299
00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:39.495
<v VINCENT>Yes.

300
00:22:39.495 --> 00:22:41.124
<v ARSENEAULT>They did?

301
00:22:41.124 --> 00:22:41.938
<v VINCENT>Yes.

302
00:22:41.938 --> 00:22:55.784
My father went to school in D.C. and that's where he learned his trade as a builder.

303
00:22:55.784 --> 00:23:00.670
And we were taught to build.

304
00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kinds of stories would they tell you?

305
00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:19.532
<v VINCENT>Basically how hard it was because they had to work on the farm so they were doing farm work for their daddy.

306
00:23:19.532 --> 00:23:28.968
They would tell stories how they would sneak off to the swimming hole, go swimming and, you know.

307
00:23:28.968 --> 00:23:37.355
In other words little stuff they were doing that they wasn't supposed to do, you know.

308
00:23:37.355 --> 00:23:41.548
And my father was . . . He was very creative.

309
00:23:41.548 --> 00:23:53.081
I think I told y'all he built a roller coaster before he got into his . . . Before he got out of his teens.

310
00:23:53.081 --> 00:23:58.323
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, so he was a teenager when he built it?

311
00:23:58.323 --> 00:23:58.847
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

312
00:23:58.847 --> 00:23:59.371
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

313
00:23:59.371 --> 00:24:01.468
I didn't know that.

314
00:24:01.468 --> 00:24:08.282
<v VINCENT>And he built a house before he . . . while he was a teenager too.

315
00:24:08.282 --> 00:24:08.806
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

316
00:24:08.806 --> 00:24:11.952
So he was just naturally gifted…

317
00:24:11.952 --> 00:24:13.000
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

318
00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:13.925
<v ARSENEAULT>…like that?

319
00:24:13.925 --> 00:24:14.388
<v VINCENT>Yes.

320
00:24:14.388 --> 00:24:19.015
Well most of my family were in the building business.

321
00:24:19.015 --> 00:24:20.403
Carpentry and whatnot.

322
00:24:20.403 --> 00:24:27.343
Yeah, the community was amazed and they still talk about that today, the older people.

323
00:24:27.343 --> 00:24:29.194
Did you talk to . . .?

324
00:24:29.194 --> 00:24:32.896
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean they're amazed by the roller coaster?

325
00:24:32.896 --> 00:24:33.358
<v VINCENT>Yes.

326
00:24:33.358 --> 00:24:34.746
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was it?

327
00:24:34.746 --> 00:24:39.836
<v VINCENT>Up on the piece of land I was telling you about.

328
00:24:39.836 --> 00:24:41.687
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh the Krause &amp; Managan?

329
00:24:41.687 --> 00:24:43.075
<v VINCENT>The big piece.

330
00:24:43.075 --> 00:24:44.000
Yeah.

331
00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:45.892
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of roller coaster was it?

332
00:24:45.892 --> 00:24:47.243
What did it look like?

333
00:24:47.243 --> 00:24:48.324
<v VINCENT>A wooden roller coaster.

334
00:24:48.324 --> 00:24:48.595
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

335
00:24:48.595 --> 00:24:49.946
Did it go upside down?

336
00:24:49.946 --> 00:24:50.486
<v VINCENT>Oh, no.

337
00:24:50.486 --> 00:24:51.568
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay I'm just wondering.

338
00:24:51.568 --> 00:24:52.378
I don't know.

339
00:24:52.378 --> 00:24:54.000
<v VINCENT>It was a wooden one.

340
00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.931
<v ARSENEAULT>Are there any pictures anywhere?

341
00:24:56.931 --> 00:24:59.862
<v VINCENT>Not that I know of.

342
00:24:59.862 --> 00:25:04.552
I would like to see the pictures myself.

343
00:25:04.552 --> 00:25:06.897
<v ARSENEAULT>That would be awesome.

344
00:25:06.897 --> 00:25:11.000
<v VINCENT>So that was part of the history.

345
00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:14.517
<v ARSENEAULT>Anything else they would tell y'all?

346
00:25:14.517 --> 00:25:18.621
<v VINCENT>We had a cousin named Pete Moss.

347
00:25:18.621 --> 00:25:22.138
There's a picture of him somewhere.

348
00:25:22.138 --> 00:25:22.724
Did

349
00:25:22.724 --> 00:25:28.000
<c.p>anybody ever tell you about the Moss family?

350
00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:29.965
<v ARSENEAULT>I've heard some things.

351
00:25:29.965 --> 00:25:37.827
<v VINCENT>But he had a horse and buggy and somebody got a picture of him . . . Oh no,

352
00:25:37.827 --> 00:25:44.705
it was in the newspaper . . . Going across that bridge with the horse and buggy.

353
00:25:44.705 --> 00:25:48.145
That old bridge that y'all come across.

354
00:25:48.145 --> 00:25:48.636
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

355
00:25:48.636 --> 00:25:50.601
<v VINCENT>Can you imagine that?

356
00:25:50.601 --> 00:25:51.584
<v ARSENEAULT>No. Cannot.

357
00:25:51.584 --> 00:25:52.075
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

358
00:25:52.075 --> 00:26:00.919
<v ARSENEAULT>Think I would feel like I was in a dream if I saw a horse and buggy on . . .

359
00:26:00.919 --> 00:26:05.341
<v VINCENT>Yeah that was amazing, too, to hear that story.

360
00:26:05.341 --> 00:26:09.763
But the people lived just simple here, you know.

361
00:26:09.763 --> 00:26:10.746
Simple living.

362
00:26:10.746 --> 00:26:14.676
So he was one of my good friends.

363
00:26:14.676 --> 00:26:17.133
His brother came to Washington.

364
00:26:17.133 --> 00:26:20.572
My father encouraged him to come there.

365
00:26:20.572 --> 00:26:25.977
He got a job at the Bureau of Engraving making money.

366
00:26:25.977 --> 00:26:35.803
And he used to come back and visit and had a pocket full of change he would give the children.

367
00:26:35.803 --> 00:26:38.260
They thought he was rich.

368
00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:40.225
He was dressed nice.

369
00:26:40.225 --> 00:26:43.173
Suit and all that stuff, you

370
00:26:43.173 --> 00:26:43.665
<c.p>know.

371
00:26:43.665 --> 00:26:49.561
So when he came in they were looking forward to seeing him.

372
00:26:49.561 --> 00:26:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They were probably getting some money.

373
00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:53.540
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

374
00:26:53.540 --> 00:26:56.780
<v ARSENEAULT>They probably thought he made money.

375
00:26:56.780 --> 00:26:57.320
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

376
00:26:57.320 --> 00:27:00.020
They though the made money.

377
00:27:00.020 --> 00:27:05.420
He told them, "I work at the Bureau of Engraving.

378
00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:07.040
We make money."

379
00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:12.440
You know so, oh, they were all excited about that.

380
00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:13.520
<v ARSENEAULT>That's funny.

381
00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:15.680
<v VINCENT>So let me see.

382
00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:16.760
Anything else?

383
00:27:16.760 --> 00:27:25.400
Main thing, they did a lot of hard work and most of them did carpentry work.

384
00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:27.020
And farm work.

385
00:27:27.020 --> 00:27:31.340
But they helped a lot of people here.

386
00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:32.960
Everybody knew them.

387
00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:34.580
Everybody respected them.

388
00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:41.060
And I think I told y'all this too, he used to ride.

389
00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:43.760
He was a big man.

390
00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:45.380
Big and tall.

391
00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Eli?

392
00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:57.411
<v VINCENT>Yeah, and he used to ride a white horse and he carried a whip with him everywhere he'd go.

393
00:27:57.411 --> 00:27:59.603
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was he whipping?

394
00:27:59.603 --> 00:28:03.986
<v VINCENT>Anybody that got out of order with him.

395
00:28:03.986 --> 00:28:07.822
Messed with him, you know, or whatever.

396
00:28:07.822 --> 00:28:12.205
Didn't matter whether he was black or white.

397
00:28:12.205 --> 00:28:28.644
Seemed like I kind of took after him because when I came here I rode a bicycle a lot and I always had a machete with me on the bike.

398
00:28:28.644 --> 00:28:32.479
And the children were scared of me.

399
00:28:32.479 --> 00:28:46.726
Run in the house say, "Mama the man out there passing by with a machete." [Laughter] So they were amused with me when I came here,

400
00:28:46.726 --> 00:28:54.397
because I was doing a lot of farm bush working and whatnot, you know.

401
00:28:54.397 --> 00:28:56.041
But mostly work.

402
00:28:56.041 --> 00:29:00.973
My father was a no nonsense man, you know.

403
00:29:00.973 --> 00:29:01.521
So . . .

404
00:29:01.521 --> 00:29:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of values did he instill in you?

405
00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.069
<v VINCENT>Morals and principles.

406
00:29:09.069 --> 00:29:13.207
He was a religious type man.

407
00:29:13.207 --> 00:29:22.862
He was a Baptist so we had to go to church on Sunday mornings.

408
00:29:22.862 --> 00:29:27.000
Necktie, suit and I hated that.

409
00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:31.828
So we had to go to church.

410
00:29:31.828 --> 00:29:33.207
<v ARSENEAULT>Every Sunday?

411
00:29:33.207 --> 00:29:34.586
<v VINCENT>Every Sunday.

412
00:29:34.586 --> 00:29:36.655
Every Sunday morning.

413
00:29:36.655 --> 00:29:42.172
Especially the Sunday school and whatnot you know.

414
00:29:42.172 --> 00:29:51.138
So he was a . . . He had very great morals and principles about him.

415
00:29:51.138 --> 00:29:53.207
He was fair.

416
00:29:53.207 --> 00:30:01.483
I was amazed that what he paid us as children for working.

417
00:30:01.483 --> 00:30:04.241
He paid us good.

418
00:30:04.241 --> 00:30:10.448
He used to put the cash in our envelope.

419
00:30:10.448 --> 00:30:15.276
Had an envelope for everybody that worked.

420
00:30:15.276 --> 00:30:17.345
Name on it.

421
00:30:17.345 --> 00:30:18.034
Nice . . .

422
00:30:18.034 --> 00:30:20.793
<v ARSENEAULT>Treated y'all like adults.

423
00:30:20.793 --> 00:30:24.241
<v VINCENT>So he was very good.

424
00:30:24.241 --> 00:30:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How many siblings?

425
00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:31.218
<v VINCENT>It was eight of us all together.

426
00:30:31.218 --> 00:30:37.846
A half-brother and we didn't know him until later on though.

427
00:30:37.846 --> 00:30:41.462
We didn’t know he was creeping.

428
00:30:41.462 --> 00:30:43.269
<v ARSENEAULT>He was what?

429
00:30:43.269 --> 00:30:44.474
<v VINCENT>Creeping. [Laughs]

430
00:30:44.474 --> 00:30:46.282
<v ARSENEAULT>He was creeping?

431
00:30:46.282 --> 00:30:48.692
He creeped on y'all?

432
00:30:48.692 --> 00:30:50.500
<v VINCENT>On our mother.

433
00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:57.128
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh y'all didn't know your father was creeping on your mama?

434
00:30:57.128 --> 00:30:58.333
I see.

435
00:30:58.333 --> 00:31:02.551
I thought you meant your step brother.

436
00:31:02.551 --> 00:31:14.000
<v VINCENT>Yeah he . . . So he had a son a little maybe about two months older than my oldest sister.

437
00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:15.696
<v ARSENEAULT>[Laughs] Family drama.

438
00:31:15.696 --> 00:31:16.261
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

439
00:31:16.261 --> 00:31:17.957
<v ARSENEAULT>It's always interesting.

440
00:31:17.957 --> 00:31:18.522
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

441
00:31:18.522 --> 00:31:25.304
And when I first met him, it wasn't until I moved here.

442
00:31:25.304 --> 00:31:30.391
Only one that knew him was my oldest brother.

443
00:31:30.391 --> 00:31:33.783
We didn't know anything about it.

444
00:31:33.783 --> 00:31:43.391
But when I moved here I came home one day and he was standing outside the gate.

445
00:31:43.391 --> 00:31:50.174
At that time now, the gate was across where the street is.

446
00:31:50.174 --> 00:31:55.261
So when I was driving up I saw him.

447
00:31:55.261 --> 00:32:00.348
When I got close to him blew my mind.

448
00:32:00.348 --> 00:32:03.739
I thought it was my daddy.

449
00:32:03.739 --> 00:32:08.261
That's how much he looked like my daddy.

450
00:32:08.261 --> 00:32:08.826
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

451
00:32:08.826 --> 00:32:09.957
<v VINCENT>Same size.

452
00:32:09.957 --> 00:32:12.217
Same features and everything.

453
00:32:12.217 --> 00:32:19.000
We became best friends because he looked so much like my father.

454
00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:28.043
And I was my father's supervisor over there once I got up to about maybe sixteen.

455
00:32:28.043 --> 00:32:41.043
He put me in charge of jobs and making sure everybody was doing what they were supposed to do while he was gone.

456
00:32:41.043 --> 00:32:47.261
He may have been giving estimates on more jobs you know.

457
00:32:47.261 --> 00:32:58.000
So he had to leave and he put me in charge of making sure the work got done.

458
00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:03.176
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow that's a lot of responsibility for a . . .

459
00:33:03.176 --> 00:33:05.118
<v VINCENT>Yeah I was . . .

460
00:33:05.118 --> 00:33:07.059
<v ARSENEAULT>…a sixteen year-old.

461
00:33:07.059 --> 00:33:07.706
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

462
00:33:07.706 --> 00:33:12.882
But I was . . . He knew I was dependable.

463
00:33:12.882 --> 00:33:24.529
I was more dependable than my brothers because I was interested in what I was learning, you know.

464
00:33:24.529 --> 00:33:34.882
So I was the supervisor on the job and I had my cousin working for us.

465
00:33:34.882 --> 00:33:36.824
He was painting.

466
00:33:36.824 --> 00:33:39.412
He was a painter.

467
00:33:39.412 --> 00:33:43.294
And I had to fire him.

468
00:33:43.294 --> 00:33:47.824
<v ARSENEAULT>You fired him when you were sixteen?

469
00:33:47.824 --> 00:34:11.765
<v VINCENT>Yeah, because he was goofing off and I told him get busy and he didn't like the idea that I was sixteen and in charge and he probably was maybe in his thirties or forties, you know.

470
00:34:11.765 --> 00:34:18.235
So he figured he didn't have to listen to me.

471
00:34:18.235 --> 00:34:22.118
So I had to fire him.

472
00:34:22.118 --> 00:34:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he tell your dad?

473
00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:26.648
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

474
00:34:26.648 --> 00:34:37.660
But I was in charge so I was doing what I was instructed to do, you know.

475
00:34:37.660 --> 00:34:45.434
If they don't do what they supposed to do, let them go.

476
00:34:45.434 --> 00:34:49.969
So that's what I did with him.

477
00:34:49.969 --> 00:34:58.390
But I think, I don't really remember, I think he got back on.

478
00:34:58.390 --> 00:35:12.642
He learned how to follow instructions and stuff from a youngster. [phone rings] Excuse me, I got a call from Washington. [Laughs]

479
00:35:12.642 --> 00:35:13.937
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, sir.

480
00:35:13.937 --> 00:35:15.233
You ready?

481
00:35:15.233 --> 00:35:15.881
Okay.

482
00:35:15.881 --> 00:35:23.006
I forget what we were talking about before your daughter called.

483
00:35:23.006 --> 00:35:24.950
Oh my goodness.

484
00:35:24.950 --> 00:35:32.723
We were talking about Mr. Eli for a little while and then

485
00:35:32.723 --> 00:35:34.667
<c.p>I don't remember.

486
00:35:34.667 --> 00:35:42.440
Do you mind if I ask another question and then we can . . .?

487
00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:44.384
<v VINCENT>No, go ahead.

488
00:35:44.384 --> 00:35:45.031
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

489
00:35:45.031 --> 00:35:54.748
I was going to ask you when you got involved with the Black Panther Party?

490
00:35:54.748 --> 00:35:56.692
<v VINCENT>In the sixties.

491
00:35:56.692 --> 00:35:59.283
Around sixty . . . About sixty-seven.

492
00:35:59.283 --> 00:35:59.931
Sixty-six.

493
00:35:59.931 --> 00:36:02.522
Somewhere around in there.

494
00:36:02.522 --> 00:36:03.818
<v ARSENEAULT>And why?

495
00:36:03.818 --> 00:36:09.000
Do you remember why you got involved?

496
00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:19.648
<v VINCENT>Yeah, because of the need to right all the wrongs that was going on at that time.

497
00:36:19.648 --> 00:36:28.418
And from my experience in the military and how I was treated in there.

498
00:36:28.418 --> 00:36:31.549
So that's the main reason.

499
00:36:31.549 --> 00:36:40.319
Because a lot was going on then, as you see in those articles there.

500
00:36:40.319 --> 00:36:40.945
So . . .

501
00:36:40.945 --> 00:36:41.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Definitely.

502
00:36:41.571 --> 00:36:45.330
Is this you in this article?

503
00:36:45.330 --> 00:36:51.593
<v VINCENT>Yeah that's me coming out the house after that three-hour . . .

504
00:36:51.593 --> 00:36:54.099
<v ARSENEAULT>Which one is you?

505
00:36:54.099 --> 00:36:59.110
<v VINCENT>The one with the towel over the face.

506
00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:00.363
<v ARSENEAULT>Right here?

507
00:37:00.363 --> 00:37:00.989
<v VINCENT>Yes.

508
00:37:00.989 --> 00:37:04.747
And these two are my brothers.

509
00:37:04.747 --> 00:37:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

510
00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:12.564
<v VINCENT>When we . . . When I came out we got in the police car.

511
00:37:12.564 --> 00:37:14.752
This is a lawyer.

512
00:37:14.752 --> 00:37:18.581
I think his name was [Dicasso Mason?].

513
00:37:18.581 --> 00:37:26.239
He was in the front seat with the cop that was driving the car.

514
00:37:26.239 --> 00:37:30.068
I was in the back seat and . . .

515
00:37:30.068 --> 00:37:32.256
<v ARSENEAULT>This was in 1968?

516
00:37:32.256 --> 00:37:32.803
<v VINCENT>Yes.

517
00:37:32.803 --> 00:37:33.350
<v ARSENEAULT>1968.

518
00:37:33.350 --> 00:37:36.085
<v VINCENT>No, no that was nineteen . . .

519
00:37:36.085 --> 00:37:37.179
<v ARSENEAULT>That's seventy.

520
00:37:37.179 --> 00:37:38.274
<v VINCENT>Seventy, yes.

521
00:37:38.274 --> 00:37:50.308
And then we went downtown to court and they were charging me with I think nine . . . It was nine . . . What was it?

522
00:37:50.308 --> 00:37:53.043
I don't remember the number.

523
00:37:53.043 --> 00:37:58.513
But anyway, it was about five cops at my door.

524
00:37:58.513 --> 00:38:00.154
Something like that.

525
00:38:00.154 --> 00:38:04.530
And they were charging me with account of

526
00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:10.000
<c.p>assault on each cop that was at my door.

527
00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:15.303
<v ARSENEAULT>I think you have a call coming in.

528
00:38:15.303 --> 00:38:24.584
So you were talking about your memories of being in the Black Panther Party.

529
00:38:24.584 --> 00:38:26.573
<v VINCENT>I think . . . Okay.

530
00:38:26.573 --> 00:38:37.180
We were teaching people . . . Well what our job was to teach people to defend their selves.

531
00:38:37.180 --> 00:38:42.483
So we were teaching people how to shoot.

532
00:38:42.483 --> 00:38:48.449
And that's why they did what they did there.

533
00:38:48.449 --> 00:39:05.022
That's why they broke in on me, you know, because of the work we were doing with the community and the people in the community.

534
00:39:05.022 --> 00:39:19.607
They didn't want them to have knowledge of self and how to protect their selves and what their rights are and stuff.

535
00:39:19.607 --> 00:39:24.910
So were teaching them that kind of stuff.

536
00:39:24.910 --> 00:39:27.562
That's what was happening.

537
00:39:27.562 --> 00:39:36.180
And that's one reason they came to my house the way they did.

538
00:39:36.180 --> 00:39:40.820
And they had a warrant for drugs.

539
00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:45.461
They claimed it was a drug raid.

540
00:39:45.461 --> 00:40:00.045
But as your see one of the articles say they came in looking for drugs but all they got was slugs. [laughs]

541
00:40:00.045 --> 00:40:03.360
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh as in like shooting?

542
00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:04.685
<v VINCENT>Yeah so . . .

543
00:40:04.685 --> 00:40:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You shot at them?

544
00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:10.398
<v VINCENT>I shot one of them.

545
00:40:10.398 --> 00:40:23.347
So I went . . . I think we were talking about . . . I went to court . . . That’s pictures when they were taking me to the court house after the shootout.

546
00:40:23.347 --> 00:40:25.745
<c.p>You didn't see that article.

547
00:40:25.745 --> 00:40:35.816
But so they were charging me with, I think it was nine counts of assault on however many cops there was.

548
00:40:35.816 --> 00:40:40.133
How many cops it say was at the door?

549
00:40:40.133 --> 00:40:42.531
I believe it was five.

550
00:40:42.531 --> 00:40:43.969
Something like that.

551
00:40:43.969 --> 00:40:48.765
But I went before this judge called Harry T. Alexander.

552
00:40:48.765 --> 00:40:50.204
A black man.

553
00:40:50.204 --> 00:40:53.082
And they was telling that story.

554
00:40:53.082 --> 00:40:58.837
The D. A. [District Attorney] was telling, "Oh, he shot a cop.

555
00:40:58.837 --> 00:41:01.714
He's a threat to the community.”

556
00:41:01.714 --> 00:41:04.112
This, that and the other.

557
00:41:04.112 --> 00:41:08.429
The judge is sitting up there listening to him.

558
00:41:08.429 --> 00:41:13.704
The judge knew that they had shot me. [terrible noise interference]

559
00:41:13.704 --> 00:41:18.500
<v ARSENEAULT>So two years previous you had been shot by cops?

560
00:41:18.500 --> 00:41:27.612
<v VINCENT>Yeah, but I filed a suit against them for two and a half million dollars after they shot me.

561
00:41:27.612 --> 00:41:30.969
Because I was gainfully employed and whatnot.

562
00:41:30.969 --> 00:41:32.408
Working two jobs.

563
00:41:32.408 --> 00:41:38.643
I worked as a carpenter in the government which was National Capital Housing.

564
00:41:38.643 --> 00:41:41.041
They call it the projects.

565
00:41:41.041 --> 00:41:42.480
Like when they . . .

566
00:41:42.480 --> 00:41:45.357
<v ARSENEAULT>They already knew who you were?

567
00:41:45.357 --> 00:41:48.714
The judge already knew who you were?

568
00:41:48.714 --> 00:41:49.194
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

569
00:41:49.194 --> 00:42:01.663
So the judge let me out on personal bond and they were really pissed off then because the judge knew that they were out of order.

570
00:42:01.663 --> 00:42:16.531
So and I think y'all can do the research yourself but I think I'm probably the only one here in this country shot a cop and got out on personal bond.

571
00:42:16.531 --> 00:42:27.561
And when I came back to the community the whole community, I mean the whole house, was surrounded by people in the community.

572
00:42:27.561 --> 00:42:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>In Washington?

573
00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:29.769
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

574
00:42:29.769 --> 00:42:39.769
And when I came home and got out the car saluted, everybody cheered.

575
00:42:39.769 --> 00:42:44.385
<v ARSENEAULT>Bet that was a good feeling.

576
00:42:44.385 --> 00:42:45.154
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

577
00:42:45.154 --> 00:42:50.538
So that was part of the history.

578
00:42:50.538 --> 00:42:59.769
<v ARSENEAULT>So what were some of your responsibilities in the Black Panther Party?

579
00:42:59.769 --> 00:43:02.077
<v VINCENT>Teaching people self-awareness.

580
00:43:02.077 --> 00:43:02.846
Consciousness.

581
00:43:02.846 --> 00:43:08.231
And teaching them how to handle firearms.

582
00:43:08.231 --> 00:43:14.385
Same types of things the military teaches people.

583
00:43:14.385 --> 00:43:17.462
How to defend yourself.

584
00:43:17.462 --> 00:43:21.308
What your rights are and . . .

585
00:43:21.308 --> 00:43:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How did you get into the Black Panther Party?

586
00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:31.235
<v VINCENT>It’s kind of deep.

587
00:43:31.235 --> 00:43:39.059
You have to be willing . . . Well one thing my history, being in the military.

588
00:43:39.059 --> 00:43:41.853
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that hard for them?

589
00:43:41.853 --> 00:43:45.206
Was that helpful to get in?

590
00:43:45.206 --> 00:43:50.235
<v VINCENT>Yeah, because and your level of consciousness, you know.

591
00:43:50.235 --> 00:43:52.471
So that's basically it.

592
00:43:52.471 --> 00:44:00.853
<c.p>And your willingness to raise the consciousness of the people of the community, you know,

593
00:44:00.853 --> 00:44:12.029
because a lot of people are not aware of what’s happening to them, because they got nine to five jobs.

594
00:44:12.029 --> 00:44:13.147
ARSENEAULT: Kids.

595
00:44:13.147 --> 00:44:13.706
Mortgage.

596
00:44:13.706 --> 00:44:14.265
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

597
00:44:14.265 --> 00:44:17.618
So that's the way it went.

598
00:44:17.618 --> 00:44:21.529
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any kind of like initiation?

599
00:44:21.529 --> 00:44:26.000
To get in or anything like that?

600
00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:36.494
<v VINCENT>Well the initiation was your level of awareness and your experience, you know.

601
00:44:36.494 --> 00:44:41.337
That was mainly it, you know.

602
00:44:41.337 --> 00:44:57.482
So the other offspring that I had, the three big bellies when I went in the military, was a boy.

603
00:44:57.482 --> 00:45:03.940
<v ARSENEAULT>So you have two daughters and a son?

604
00:45:03.940 --> 00:45:04.747
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

605
00:45:04.747 --> 00:45:07.976
<v ARSENEAULT>So it's Stephanie, Shella . . .

606
00:45:07.976 --> 00:45:12.819
<v VINCENT>And the other one's named Marvin.

607
00:45:12.819 --> 00:45:16.048
<v ARSENEAULT>After your slave name?

608
00:45:16.048 --> 00:45:17.663
<v VINCENT>Slave name.

609
00:45:17.663 --> 00:45:18.470
Yeah.

610
00:45:18.470 --> 00:45:33.000
His mother named him that so . . . He couldn't leave the drugs alone so he died at forty-seven.

611
00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:37.286
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm so sorry to hear that.

612
00:45:37.286 --> 00:45:40.857
Did he ever live here?

613
00:45:40.857 --> 00:45:47.286
<v VINCENT>No. His whole life was Washington, D.C. and drugs.

614
00:45:47.286 --> 00:46:03.000
Yeah, so where were we talking about . . . I came . . . They couldn't search my house until I went to court and came back.

615
00:46:03.000 --> 00:46:10.857
They went and searched my house, they didn't find no drugs.

616
00:46:10.857 --> 00:46:18.000
But they did . . . I had a police special thirty-eight stole.

617
00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:24.429
They stole some stuff out of my house, too.

618
00:46:24.429 --> 00:46:30.143
And I had a picture in the house.

619
00:46:30.143 --> 00:46:41.571
Y'all remember . . . Y'all might remember the history of this . . . I can't remember just where it was.

620
00:46:41.571 --> 00:46:52.286
These three brothers went in . . . What was . . . I don't know what baseball, basketball, or what.

621
00:46:52.286 --> 00:47:03.000
But anyway, they won the game and you know how they get on the podium.

622
00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:08.714
I'm trying to remember what they were doing.

623
00:47:08.714 --> 00:47:12.286
<v ARSENEAULT>Like the Olympics or something?

624
00:47:12.286 --> 00:47:21.571
<v VINCENT>It may have been the Olympics but anyway they had on black gloves.

625
00:47:21.571 --> 00:47:24.429
Y'all remember that picture?

626
00:47:24.429 --> 00:47:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I've seen that picture.

627
00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:29.031
<v VINCENT>You've seen it?

628
00:47:29.031 --> 00:47:31.438
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm pretty sure I've seen it yeah.

629
00:47:31.438 --> 00:47:31.781
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

630
00:47:31.781 --> 00:47:36.594
Well that was on my wall when they shot at me in my house.

631
00:47:36.594 --> 00:47:43.125
Like I was in the hallway and the picture was on this wall and I was standing over here.

632
00:47:43.125 --> 00:47:46.562
And when they first shot they shot, hit that picture.

633
00:47:46.562 --> 00:47:46.906
Yeah.

634
00:47:46.906 --> 00:47:48.281
<v ARSENEAULT>You still have it?

635
00:47:48.281 --> 00:47:50.688
<v VINCENT>I don't know what happened to it.

636
00:47:50.688 --> 00:47:58.938
But that was a historical picture because they were mad at those brothers for having black gloves on and saluting you know, the struggle.

637
00:47:58.938 --> 00:48:00.312
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that black power?

638
00:48:00.312 --> 00:48:01.000
The . . .

639
00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:01.524
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

640
00:48:01.524 --> 00:48:04.143
That's the salute, you know.

641
00:48:04.143 --> 00:48:15.143
So they were really mad about that at the Olympics or whatever it was I can't remember just what it was.

642
00:48:15.143 --> 00:48:18.810
But that was a historical picture, too.

643
00:48:18.810 --> 00:48:21.429
You want to finish this?

644
00:48:21.429 --> 00:48:24.048
<v KYLE TANGLAO>Can I move this or . . .?

645
00:48:24.048 --> 00:48:25.095
<v VINCENT>It'll be . . .

646
00:48:25.095 --> 00:48:29.810
<v ARSENEAULT>You want me to scoot back so you can . . .?

647
00:48:29.810 --> 00:48:32.429
<v VINCENT>It'll be difficult to move.

648
00:48:32.429 --> 00:48:35.048
You can move back some.

649
00:48:35.048 --> 00:48:37.667
<v ARSENEAULT>That way you can sit.

650
00:48:37.667 --> 00:48:39.238
Oh, I'm sweating.

651
00:48:39.238 --> 00:48:42.381
I sweated all over your chair.

652
00:48:42.381 --> 00:48:45.000
<v VINCENT>Don't worry about it.

653
00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:50.141
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm just going to sit right here.

654
00:48:50.141 --> 00:48:50.875
Okay.

655
00:48:50.875 --> 00:49:06.297
So do you maybe want to talk a little bit about how the Black Panther Party has changed throughout the years?

656
00:49:06.297 --> 00:49:11.438
<v VINCENT>Yeah it got a little more mellow.

657
00:49:11.438 --> 00:49:14.375
You remember Angela Davis?

658
00:49:14.375 --> 00:49:18.781
<v ARSENEAULT>I remember you talking about her.

659
00:49:18.781 --> 00:49:25.391
<v VINCENT>Oh, but you don't know nothing about her history?

660
00:49:25.391 --> 00:49:27.594
<v ARSENEAULT>A little bit.

661
00:49:27.594 --> 00:49:28.328
<v VINCENT>Okay.

662
00:49:28.328 --> 00:49:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you meet her?

663
00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:34.586
<v VINCENT>No, I didn't meet her.

664
00:49:34.586 --> 00:49:37.690
I really wanted to meet her.

665
00:49:37.690 --> 00:49:41.828
I was in love with that sister there.

666
00:49:41.828 --> 00:49:43.379
She was bad.

667
00:49:43.379 --> 00:49:47.000
Did you remember how her history went?

668
00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:51.655
<v ARSENEAULT>Was she the one who got arrested in California?

669
00:49:51.655 --> 00:49:52.172
Yeah?

670
00:49:52.172 --> 00:49:55.276
And she was very vocal about . . .

671
00:49:55.276 --> 00:49:58.379
<v VINCENT>She had a big old afro.

672
00:49:58.379 --> 00:49:59.414
<v ARSENEAULT>She did?

673
00:49:59.414 --> 00:49:59.931
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

674
00:49:59.931 --> 00:50:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I remember that.

675
00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:02.686
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

676
00:50:02.686 --> 00:50:06.805
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about her?

677
00:50:06.805 --> 00:50:15.728
<v VINCENT>How vocal she was, how fine she was, and how active she was.

678
00:50:15.728 --> 00:50:20.533
She was in college at that time.

679
00:50:20.533 --> 00:50:30.142
And then she end up in the courthouse shootout where they killed the judge.

680
00:50:30.142 --> 00:50:32.201
Y'all remember that?

681
00:50:32.201 --> 00:50:35.633
That was in California, too.

682
00:50:35.633 --> 00:50:37.692
You remember it?

683
00:50:37.692 --> 00:50:45.929
What happened, they were getting ready . . . They were breaking Jonathan Jackson out.

684
00:50:45.929 --> 00:50:48.675
That was her man.

685
00:50:48.675 --> 00:50:56.225
And they bust up in the courtroom, took the judge hostage.

686
00:50:56.225 --> 00:51:01.030
They taped a shotgun to his head.

687
00:51:01.030 --> 00:51:08.580
And the shotgun tape . . . So he couldn't move or get away.

688
00:51:08.580 --> 00:51:14.757
They took him out and ended up killing him.

689
00:51:14.757 --> 00:51:19.562
But a few others got killed, too.

690
00:51:19.562 --> 00:51:25.740
They had him in a car, taking him somewhere.

691
00:51:25.740 --> 00:51:29.172
And they had a shootout.

692
00:51:29.172 --> 00:51:31.231
Killed the judge.

693
00:51:31.231 --> 00:51:38.781
So it was a lot of activity going on back then.

694
00:51:38.781 --> 00:51:41.527
People was resisting that

695
00:51:41.527 --> 00:51:44.959
<c.p>exploitation and oppression, you know.

696
00:51:44.959 --> 00:51:49.763
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, that was a very busy time.

697
00:51:49.763 --> 00:51:50.450
<v VINCENT>Very.

698
00:51:50.450 --> 00:51:51.136
Yeah.

699
00:51:51.136 --> 00:51:53.195
Yeah, very, very.

700
00:51:53.195 --> 00:51:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was the movement the strongest?

701
00:51:58.000 --> 00:51:59.500
<v VINCENT>I don't know.

702
00:51:59.500 --> 00:52:03.000
It was about the same all over.

703
00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:06.500
The east coast and the west coast.

704
00:52:06.500 --> 00:52:07.500
You know?

705
00:52:07.500 --> 00:52:11.500
And a few places in between, you know.

706
00:52:11.500 --> 00:52:13.500
But everybody was active.

707
00:52:13.500 --> 00:52:20.000
They had black party panther chapters in a whole lot of different places.

708
00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:26.000
But the most active was the east coast and the west coast.

709
00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:29.000
You want to try your hand?

710
00:52:29.000 --> 00:52:29.500
<v TANGLAO>Sure.

711
00:52:29.500 --> 00:52:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So . . .

712
00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:37.500
<v VINCENT>The other side is already laid out if you want to do the other side.

713
00:52:37.500 --> 00:52:39.500
I'll finish this side.

714
00:52:39.500 --> 00:52:42.500
You can do the other side.

715
00:52:42.500 --> 00:52:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about Martin Luther King?

716
00:52:47.000 --> 00:52:52.586
<v VINCENT>Well we were supporting Martin Luther King, but our major freedom

717
00:52:52.586 --> 00:53:03.759
<c.p>fighter was Malcolm X. We didn't have whole lot of interest in Martin Luther King because he was selected by the system.

718
00:53:03.759 --> 00:53:14.932
He was . . . In actuality he was a traitor to the struggle because he was following the orders of the system, you know.

719
00:53:14.932 --> 00:53:21.026
So but we wasn't going to let them do nothing to him.

720
00:53:21.026 --> 00:53:25.597
But we didn't follow that turn the other cheek.

721
00:53:25.597 --> 00:53:28.644
That wasn't what we were about.

722
00:53:28.644 --> 00:53:32.707
We were about defending ourselves and our rights.

723
00:53:32.707 --> 00:53:38.801
He was on the other side. [Ralph David] Abernathy, you remember Abernathy?

724
00:53:38.801 --> 00:53:41.340
<v ARSENEAULT>That name sounds really familiar.

725
00:53:41.340 --> 00:53:48.958
<v VINCENT>He was with Martin Luther King, but he was more active than Martin Luther King.

726
00:53:48.958 --> 00:53:53.021
More vocal in everything than Martin Luther King.

727
00:53:53.021 --> 00:54:01.654
So they wouldn't let him come to the forefront, you know, they kept him in the background.

728
00:54:01.654 --> 00:54:07.241
I guess that their little organization didn't want radicalness in it.

729
00:54:07.241 --> 00:54:18.414
They wanted to turn the other cheek, turn the other cheek, let them kick you in the butt and all that stuff.

730
00:54:18.414 --> 00:54:21.461
<v ARSENEAULT>That's not how the Panther Party . . .?

731
00:54:21.461 --> 00:54:21.969
<v VINCENT>No.

732
00:54:21.969 --> 00:54:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . worked at all?

733
00:54:24.000 --> 00:54:24.640
<v VINCENT>No.

734
00:54:24.640 --> 00:54:31.680
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some of the other things that the party did?

735
00:54:31.680 --> 00:54:33.600
<v VINCENT>Fed the people.

736
00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:39.360
Taught them how to stand up for their rights.

737
00:54:39.360 --> 00:54:44.480
Helped them in any way they needed help.

738
00:54:44.480 --> 00:54:47.040
Protected them, you know.

739
00:54:47.040 --> 00:54:53.440
You know they had a breakfast program for the children.

740
00:54:53.440 --> 00:54:56.640
<v ARSENEAULT>Do they still do that?

741
00:54:56.640 --> 00:54:58.560
<v VINCENT>I don't believe.

742
00:54:58.560 --> 00:55:10.080
But you know, the government came in and they got the provocateurs come in and disrupt you, so.

743
00:55:10.080 --> 00:55:17.120
In other words, they don't want to see no active organization.

744
00:55:17.120 --> 00:55:20.960
You know, they didn't want that.

745
00:55:20.960 --> 00:55:28.640
They wanted everybody to be passive and go along with slavery, basically.

746
00:55:28.640 --> 00:55:40.160
So we were about standing up, fighting for our rights, whatever was necessary to be who we are.

747
00:55:40.160 --> 00:55:51.680
They wanted us to be who they wanted us to be and what they wanted us to be.

748
00:55:51.680 --> 00:55:57.440
And that's basically working on some plantation for them.

749
00:55:57.440 --> 00:56:00.000
For the government.

750
00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:07.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have a most vivid memory from your time when you were active in the party?

751
00:56:07.667 --> 00:56:09.922
Or are you still active?

752
00:56:09.922 --> 00:56:14.431
I don't want to say that you're not still active.

753
00:56:14.431 --> 00:56:14.882
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

754
00:56:14.882 --> 00:56:20.745
<v ARSENEAULT>Or I guess the sixties were such an interesting time in our history.

755
00:56:20.745 --> 00:56:25.706
I was just wondering if you had a most vivid memory.

756
00:56:25.706 --> 00:56:31.569
You have so many like experiences but maybe one that sticks out more.

757
00:56:31.569 --> 00:56:42.843
<v VINCENT>One of them was we, I think it was in Philly, we went to Philadelphia because they were exploiting the people up there real bad.

758
00:56:42.843 --> 00:56:45.098
They took the active brothers.

759
00:56:45.098 --> 00:56:49.157
I don't remember how many of them there were.

760
00:56:49.157 --> 00:56:52.314
Maybe about six or eight of them.

761
00:56:52.314 --> 00:57:00.431
Made them strip buck naked up against the wall and they had a picture of them, you know.

762
00:57:00.431 --> 00:57:06.745
So at that time it was a picture out of who’s the president then?

763
00:57:06.745 --> 00:57:07.196
<v ARSENEAULT>Kennedy?

764
00:57:07.196 --> 00:57:08.098
<v VINCENT>Ronald Reagan?

765
00:57:08.098 --> 00:57:09.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Johnson?

766
00:57:09.000 --> 00:57:10.016
<v VINCENT>No. Dick.

767
00:57:10.016 --> 00:57:11.033
Tricky Dick.

768
00:57:11.033 --> 00:57:12.557
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, Richard Nixon?

769
00:57:12.557 --> 00:57:13.066
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

770
00:57:13.066 --> 00:57:16.623
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't know my dates too well.

771
00:57:16.623 --> 00:57:17.639
<v VINCENT>I think . . .

772
00:57:17.639 --> 00:57:22.213
<v ARSENEAULT>I know Kennedy and Johnson are in there somewhere.

773
00:57:22.213 --> 00:57:26.279
<v VINCENT>Yeah, but I think it was Tricky Dick.

774
00:57:26.279 --> 00:57:39.492
But anyway, whichever one it was they had his . . . They had a picture of either Huey Newton or Bobby Seal in the electric chair, you know.

775
00:57:39.492 --> 00:57:45.590
In the electric chair, and they had his picture on the picture.

776
00:57:45.590 --> 00:57:49.148
Where they had him in the picture.

777
00:57:49.148 --> 00:57:58.295
But what we did we cut the face, his face off and put Tricky Dick's face on there.

778
00:57:58.295 --> 00:58:00.836
We made some big posters.

779
00:58:00.836 --> 00:58:07.951
We made about a hundred of them because we used to do silkscreen work.

780
00:58:07.951 --> 00:58:09.475
You know silkscreen?

781
00:58:09.475 --> 00:58:11.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Like t-shirts?

782
00:58:11.000 --> 00:58:11.607
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

783
00:58:11.607 --> 00:58:14.037
You know, by hand.

784
00:58:14.037 --> 00:58:24.972
So we made about a hundred of those and went to Philly or wherever it was that happened.

785
00:58:24.972 --> 00:58:28.009
It was Philadelphia, I think.

786
00:58:28.009 --> 00:58:30.439
And to a rally.

787
00:58:30.439 --> 00:58:37.121
And when we pulled those pictures out they went like hotcakes.

788
00:58:37.121 --> 00:58:40.766
People bought them up like mad.

789
00:58:40.766 --> 00:58:45.019
I guess some people still got them.

790
00:58:45.019 --> 00:58:57.776
I don't know what happened to the one I had. [Laughs] But that was one of my most interesting engagements there.

791
00:58:57.776 --> 00:58:58.383
Yeah.

792
00:58:58.383 --> 00:59:03.243
<v ARSENEAULT>Who did you work with, in the party?

793
00:59:03.243 --> 00:59:06.280
<v VINCENT>Oh, I don't remember names.

794
00:59:06.280 --> 00:59:10.533
But the major players were in California.

795
00:59:10.533 --> 00:59:16.607
You know, like Huey Newton and Bobby Seal and whatnot.

796
00:59:16.607 --> 00:59:29.972
I was one of the major players in Washington and we had some players in New York and all around, you know.

797
00:59:29.972 --> 00:59:34.832
But I don't remember all of their names.

798
00:59:34.832 --> 00:59:39.084
So many activities were in all that.

799
00:59:39.084 --> 00:59:45.159
We had meetings, and rallies, and everything all the time.

800
00:59:45.159 --> 00:59:48.804
Plus we were going to demonstrations.

801
00:59:48.804 --> 00:59:54.271
Just about every demonstration they had in Washington, D.C.

802
00:59:54.271 --> 00:59:56.093
I was there.

803
00:59:56.093 --> 00:59:59.738
When Martin Luther King made his

804
00:59:59.738 --> 01:00:10.065
<c.p>speech at the . . . What building is that where they got a monument inside sitting on a chair?

805
01:00:10.065 --> 01:00:11.888
Who was that?

806
01:00:11.888 --> 01:00:13.710
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, Abraham Lincoln.

807
01:00:13.710 --> 01:00:16.140
<v VINCENT>Must have been Lincoln.

808
01:00:16.140 --> 01:00:19.785
Or one of them critters [Laughs].

809
01:00:19.785 --> 01:00:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

810
01:00:21.000 --> 01:00:25.891
<v VINCENT>But anyway I was . . . Martin Luther King spoke there.

811
01:00:25.891 --> 01:00:30.783
But I was right up front at that one.

812
01:00:30.783 --> 01:00:36.761
Most times you couldn't get close to the front, you know.

813
01:00:36.761 --> 01:00:41.652
But it was so many of those, you know.

814
01:00:41.652 --> 01:00:44.913
Everybody wanted to . . . Everybody was involved.

815
01:00:44.913 --> 01:00:48.717
That was one of the biggest ones.

816
01:00:48.717 --> 01:00:56.870
I think that may have been the one when he said, "I had a dream."

817
01:00:56.870 --> 01:00:57.413
Yeah.

818
01:00:57.413 --> 01:01:00.130
He was a good speaker.

819
01:01:00.130 --> 01:01:06.652
<v ARSENEAULT>I thought there are a lot of good speakers at that time.

820
01:01:06.652 --> 01:01:07.196
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

821
01:01:07.196 --> 01:01:09.913
<v ARSENEAULT>A lot of good orators.

822
01:01:09.913 --> 01:01:11.000
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

823
01:01:11.000 --> 01:01:13.191
<v ARSENEAULT>People spoke a little differently.

824
01:01:13.191 --> 01:01:13.630
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

825
01:01:13.630 --> 01:01:16.260
So it was so active then,

826
01:01:16.260 --> 01:01:27.655
see because I was always being approached when I had that suit against them for two and a half mil [dollars] they were always harassing me.

827
01:01:27.655 --> 01:01:28.532
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was?

828
01:01:28.532 --> 01:01:28.970
<v VINCENT>Police.

829
01:01:28.970 --> 01:01:29.847
The government.

830
01:01:29.847 --> 01:01:43.434
I had a dog that I got from a friend of mine because he was moving out of his house into an apartment and he couldn't take the dog with him.

831
01:01:43.434 --> 01:01:46.064
It was a white German Shepard.

832
01:01:46.064 --> 01:01:48.694
And he was about that big.

833
01:01:48.694 --> 01:01:57.021
So he gave him to me and he said, "I got to tell you one thing about this dog.

834
01:01:57.021 --> 01:01:59.213
He don't like white people."

835
01:01:59.213 --> 01:02:00.966
I laughed at him.

836
01:02:00.966 --> 01:02:02.719
I said, "Yeah, right.

837
01:02:02.719 --> 01:02:05.349
How he know white from black?"

838
01:02:05.349 --> 01:02:08.417
He said, "He don't like white people."

839
01:02:08.417 --> 01:02:19.374
So one day I walked him around the corner to the Safeway to do a little shopping and I tied him up outside the Safeway.

840
01:02:19.374 --> 01:02:25.511
Now this is a black community so only blacks coming in the Safeway mostly.

841
01:02:25.511 --> 01:02:30.332
And here come the bread man delivering bread to the Safeway.

842
01:02:30.332 --> 01:02:31.209
White man.

843
01:02:31.209 --> 01:02:40.851
I heard . . . I was in there shopping and I heard the dog [barking noises] sounding like he was trying to kill somebody.

844
01:02:40.851 --> 01:02:41.728
Went outside.

845
01:02:41.728 --> 01:02:43.043
White bread man . . .

846
01:02:43.043 --> 01:02:43.919
<v ARSENEAULT>White bread?

847
01:02:43.919 --> 01:02:46.987
<v VINCENT>Wouldn't let him come in the store.

848
01:02:46.987 --> 01:02:54.000
I said, that's when I found out he was telling the truth about the dog.

849
01:02:54.000 --> 01:02:54.943
<v ARSENEAULT>That's funny.

850
01:02:54.943 --> 01:02:57.771
<v VINCENT>Yeah, that was amazing to me.

851
01:02:57.771 --> 01:03:01.070
So from then on I said, okay.

852
01:03:01.070 --> 01:03:09.554
So when the police come around in the neighborhood I would bring him out on my front yard.

853
01:03:09.554 --> 01:03:18.510
Because one time they wanted to talk to me or something and I say, "Don't come on the property."

854
01:03:18.510 --> 01:03:22.280
I had him on a leash and whatnot.

855
01:03:22.280 --> 01:03:27.936
He was raising hell with the cop because the cop was white.

856
01:03:27.936 --> 01:03:31.707
So I realized brother didn't lie to me.

857
01:03:31.707 --> 01:03:35.949
So we had all kind of experiences like that.

858
01:03:35.949 --> 01:03:38.777
And it always us against them.

859
01:03:38.777 --> 01:03:43.962
One time some youngsters that we were teaching about the struggle,

860
01:03:43.962 --> 01:03:52.446
they were mad with the cops so one night the cops rode by, they shot up the car.

861
01:03:52.446 --> 01:04:04.701
And first thing they looked at was me because I lived in the community, you know, and it was about a block from where I lived.

862
01:04:04.701 --> 01:04:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The kids shot up the car?

863
01:04:08.000 --> 01:04:10.985
<v VINCENT>Shot up the police car, yeah.

864
01:04:10.985 --> 01:04:17.453
So they wanted to charge me with being involved with that, you know.

865
01:04:17.453 --> 01:04:20.935
But they couldn't make the charge stick.

866
01:04:20.935 --> 01:04:25.413
But it was always something like that going on.

867
01:04:25.413 --> 01:04:36.358
Yes, it was so much I had to really study to remember a lot of the stuff that went on, you know.

868
01:04:36.358 --> 01:04:52.279
So finally they framed me up with . . . I think I may have told y'all about this but they framed me up with an armed robbery charge of some children that had walkie-talkies.

869
01:04:52.279 --> 01:05:00.239
Some youngsters maybe a little bit older than . . . Because my daughter you just talked to Stephanie,

870
01:05:00.239 --> 01:05:09.692
she can tell you about that better than I can because they call them . . . What do they call them?

871
01:05:09.692 --> 01:05:13.672
I forgot what they call that little group.

872
01:05:13.672 --> 01:05:18.647
It may have been about eight or ten of them.

873
01:05:18.647 --> 01:05:20.139
They gave walkie-talkies.

874
01:05:20.139 --> 01:05:24.617
I'm trying to think of the name of it.

875
01:05:24.617 --> 01:05:28.100
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it for like a neighborhood group?

876
01:05:28.100 --> 01:05:39.045
<v VINCENT>Yeah, little neighborhood group that they supposed to be helping elders cross the street and walking them home and stuff like that.

877
01:05:39.045 --> 01:05:46.010
What they really were, informers telling the police what's going on in the community.

878
01:05:46.010 --> 01:05:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The kids were?

879
01:05:48.000 --> 01:05:48.461
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

880
01:05:48.461 --> 01:05:54.912
That was their job, because the police gave them walkie-talkies and whatnot and said,

881
01:05:54.912 --> 01:06:02.746
"Y'all walk people across the street and y'all report anything you see in the community, you know."

882
01:06:02.746 --> 01:06:06.433
So that happened and the cops knew me.

883
01:06:06.433 --> 01:06:13.806
And when they found out that she was working there, they charged me with robbing them.

884
01:06:13.806 --> 01:06:16.110
Just called it armed robbery.

885
01:06:16.110 --> 01:06:19.796
It wasn't a bank or anything like that.

886
01:06:19.796 --> 01:06:22.561
It was these children with walkie-talkies.

887
01:06:22.561 --> 01:06:29.473
And the way it happened is, they just took the stuff form them, you know.

888
01:06:29.473 --> 01:06:31.777
Because they were young children.

889
01:06:31.777 --> 01:06:36.846
My daughter may have been maybe twelve, thirteen, something like that.

890
01:06:36.846 --> 01:06:44.219
And she was one of them working with the . . . I can’t remember the name of it.

891
01:06:44.219 --> 01:06:52.975
But they, you know, I guess they were interviewing all of them about who took the walkie-talkies and whatnot.

892
01:06:52.975 --> 01:06:58.044
So they found out that that was my daughter working there.

893
01:06:58.044 --> 01:07:01.270
So they put the charge on me.

894
01:07:01.270 --> 01:07:13.251
But when they gave the information to the cops, the ones that took the radios from them was about a little bit older than they were.

895
01:07:13.251 --> 01:07:14.172
Maybe seventeen.

896
01:07:14.172 --> 01:07:16.016
Sixteen, seventeen, like that.

897
01:07:16.016 --> 01:07:24.771
But when they found out my daughter was working there they said, "This is a chance to get him."

898
01:07:24.771 --> 01:07:30.762
So they charged me with armed robbery of these children with these walkie-talkies.

899
01:07:30.762 --> 01:07:37.674
That's how I got four to twelve years in the . . . locked up in Loudoun, Virginia.

900
01:07:37.674 --> 01:07:42.743
And the major reason for that was because I had filed

901
01:07:42.743 --> 01:07:53.342
<c.p>that suit against for two and a half million dollars and they didn't want me to come to court clean like I was.

902
01:07:53.342 --> 01:07:54.724
No juvenile record.

903
01:07:54.724 --> 01:07:56.107
No adult record.

904
01:07:56.107 --> 01:07:58.411
You know, I was clean.

905
01:07:58.411 --> 01:08:07.627
So they said, "We got to dirty him up because he might win this two and a half million dollars.”

906
01:08:07.627 --> 01:08:10.853
So that's the way the government do.

907
01:08:10.853 --> 01:08:15.000
Set you up shoot you down you know.

908
01:08:15.000 --> 01:08:17.730
<v ARSENEAULT>So you got four years for . . .?

909
01:08:17.730 --> 01:08:22.280
<v VINCENT>I got four to twelve years right off the top.

910
01:08:22.280 --> 01:08:27.286
They wouldn't let me out on . . . In other words you know,

911
01:08:27.286 --> 01:08:38.206
usually when a person . . . They lock a person up and they don't have no record they don't lock them up they just give them . . .

912
01:08:38.206 --> 01:08:39.116
<v ARSENEAULT>They dismissed?

913
01:08:39.116 --> 01:08:41.847
It'll get dismissed or something or . . .?

914
01:08:41.847 --> 01:08:48.672
<v VINCENT>Maybe not dismissed but they’ll put them on probation or something like that, you know.

915
01:08:48.672 --> 01:08:54.587
But they put me straight in the penitentiary for four to twelve years.

916
01:08:54.587 --> 01:08:58.228
Judge found me guilty of that bogus charge.

917
01:08:58.228 --> 01:09:07.328
It was a female judge and you know, and they saw my involvement with the Panther Party so they said,

918
01:09:07.328 --> 01:09:12.788
"We got to lock him up because he's a threat to us."

919
01:09:12.788 --> 01:09:22.344
But when I went in there, they saluted me just like they did in the community when I shot the cop.

920
01:09:22.344 --> 01:09:28.714
Anything I wanted in there, all I had to do was ask for it.

921
01:09:28.714 --> 01:09:37.360
Because the guards were all walking around telling everybody, "Yeah this is the guy that like to shoot cops."

922
01:09:37.360 --> 01:09:38.270
You know?

923
01:09:38.270 --> 01:09:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm sure they loved that.

924
01:09:41.000 --> 01:09:42.686
<v VINCENT>You know that.

925
01:09:42.686 --> 01:09:46.059
I was a hero in there.

926
01:09:46.059 --> 01:09:46.621
Yeah.

927
01:09:46.621 --> 01:09:58.425
So I did five years in there and I walked out the front gate because I told my lawyer I said,

928
01:09:58.425 --> 01:10:01.797
"I ain't going back to court."

929
01:10:01.797 --> 01:10:07.418
Usually they take you to court and release you there.

930
01:10:07.418 --> 01:10:13.039
But I said, "I want to walk out this gate."

931
01:10:13.039 --> 01:10:17.536
So I set it up with my honey.

932
01:10:17.536 --> 01:10:25.405
She picked me up at the gate and that was the end of that.

933
01:10:25.405 --> 01:10:27.092
<v ARSENEAULT>What had changed?

934
01:10:27.092 --> 01:10:28.216
<v VINCENT>Oh, yeah.

935
01:10:28.216 --> 01:10:32.712
Come out to a whole different world, almost.

936
01:10:32.712 --> 01:10:45.078
You scared to mess with any female because at that time, what was it, the herpes, I think, was a thing then.

937
01:10:45.078 --> 01:10:46.203
You know?

938
01:10:46.203 --> 01:10:51.261
The people had changed and got mild, you know.

939
01:10:51.261 --> 01:10:54.634
It was a lot of change.

940
01:10:54.634 --> 01:11:03.627
Once you get locked up and come back out it's a whole lot of change involved.

941
01:11:03.627 --> 01:11:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it like adjusting?

942
01:11:07.000 --> 01:11:08.746
<v VINCENT>Adjusting back outside?

943
01:11:08.746 --> 01:11:11.656
It wasn't hard for me.

944
01:11:11.656 --> 01:11:22.714
But let me see, I got out in . . . I got out, I believe, in seventy-five or somewhere around there,

945
01:11:22.714 --> 01:11:35.519
and about I don't know how long it was, but then we went to court for the suit I had against them.

946
01:11:35.519 --> 01:11:38.429
<v ARSENEAULT>The two million dollar one?

947
01:11:38.429 --> 01:11:41.921
<v VINCENT>Yeah, two and a half million.

948
01:11:41.921 --> 01:11:52.979
And the two and a half million was never brought up in court how much I was suing for.

949
01:11:52.979 --> 01:12:06.365
I had a traitor lawyer named Peter Wolf and he crossed me because he didn't press the amount that we were suing for.

950
01:12:06.365 --> 01:12:11.021
So I end up getting a hundred and

951
01:12:11.021 --> 01:12:12.185
<c.p>thirty thousand.

952
01:12:12.185 --> 01:12:16.841
But they had this woman in there who,

953
01:12:16.841 --> 01:12:28.481
I guess she took charge of the jury and she convinced then that I didn't need that kind of money.

954
01:12:28.481 --> 01:12:34.302
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember what the jury was made up of?

955
01:12:34.302 --> 01:12:52.344
<v VINCENT>I don't remember, but I do know that one of them was a black sister and after the trial and whatnot she came to my house and told me what happened.

956
01:12:52.344 --> 01:12:54.672
So that was a . . .

957
01:12:54.672 --> 01:12:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What had happened?

958
01:12:57.000 --> 01:13:02.876
<v VINCENT>This woman who was the wife of Garfinkel or one of those big stores.

959
01:13:02.876 --> 01:13:07.073
She was the wife of the owner of the store.

960
01:13:07.073 --> 01:13:12.109
So she convinced them that I didn't need that kind of money.

961
01:13:12.109 --> 01:13:17.985
So the sister came and told me what had happened in the jury room.

962
01:13:17.985 --> 01:13:25.120
Why they were debating on what we were going to do how much and all that stuff.

963
01:13:25.120 --> 01:13:31.836
Then that woman convinced everybody on the jury that I didn't need that kind of money.

964
01:13:31.836 --> 01:13:34.354
She came and told me that.

965
01:13:34.354 --> 01:13:39.810
That just got me really pissed off more, you know, because number one,

966
01:13:39.810 --> 01:13:45.266
they didn't bring up how much it was for and you know so . . .

967
01:13:45.266 --> 01:13:46.106
<v ARSENEAULT>The lawyer?

968
01:13:46.106 --> 01:13:46.526
<v VINCENT>Lawyer.

969
01:13:46.526 --> 01:13:48.204
He was a traitor.

970
01:13:48.204 --> 01:14:00.376
Matter of fact, when he got the check he was calling me telling me the check was in and I could come pick it up and I was mad.

971
01:14:00.376 --> 01:14:03.314
I didn't go and pick it up.

972
01:14:03.314 --> 01:14:07.931
I left, he had the check for a couple of months.

973
01:14:07.931 --> 01:14:10.869
He was calling my family and everybody.

974
01:14:10.869 --> 01:14:14.646
<c.p>"Tell him the check is in, come get you."

975
01:14:14.646 --> 01:14:16.745
Because he want his money.

976
01:14:16.745 --> 01:14:21.361
So when I did go in there, what I had do,

977
01:14:21.361 --> 01:14:28.916
I went to the judge that let me out on personal bond and told him what was happening.

978
01:14:28.916 --> 01:14:29.755
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Alexander?

979
01:14:29.755 --> 01:14:30.175
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

980
01:14:30.175 --> 01:14:42.347
I went and told him that the lawyer was trying to beat me because he wanted to charge me the amount he was going to charge for appeal, too.

981
01:14:42.347 --> 01:14:44.865
But there wasn't no appeal involved.

982
01:14:44.865 --> 01:14:51.161
But he had that on his list of, you know, you know how they itemize.

983
01:14:51.161 --> 01:14:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Charges.

984
01:14:52.000 --> 01:14:52.403
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

985
01:14:52.403 --> 01:14:54.015
He had that on.

986
01:14:54.015 --> 01:15:00.060
I said, "Oh, hell no." That's why I didn't mess with the check, you know.

987
01:15:00.060 --> 01:15:08.119
So I went to Harry T. Alexander and told him, "Hey man, this lawyer trying to beat me even worse."

988
01:15:08.119 --> 01:15:11.746
So he got on the phone and called him.

989
01:15:11.746 --> 01:15:15.373
He said, "I got Mr. Vincent up in here."

990
01:15:15.373 --> 01:15:19.000
He said, "Oh tell him we got that straight.

991
01:15:19.000 --> 01:15:21.418
Tell him come get his check."

992
01:15:21.418 --> 01:15:22.627
<v ARSENEAULT>He got scared.

993
01:15:22.627 --> 01:15:25.045
The judge was talking to him.

994
01:15:25.045 --> 01:15:25.448
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

995
01:15:25.448 --> 01:15:27.463
So they straightened that out.

996
01:15:27.463 --> 01:15:29.881
I went to get my check.

997
01:15:29.881 --> 01:15:38.746
So he gave me the check and I say, "I'll go to the bank and cash it and I can pay you."

998
01:15:38.746 --> 01:15:48.821
Snatched the check out of my hand, went to the file cabinet threw it in the file cabinet and slammed it shut and locked it.

999
01:15:48.821 --> 01:15:56.881
He wanted me to sign the check and let him cash it and he going to give me my money.

1000
01:15:56.881 --> 01:15:57.687
What that

1001
01:15:57.687 --> 01:15:59.299
<c.p>sound like to you?

1002
01:15:59.299 --> 01:16:00.104
<v ARSENEAULT>A trap.

1003
01:16:00.104 --> 01:16:00.507
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1004
01:16:00.507 --> 01:16:07.358
I mean, if somebody do some work for you, do you pay them or they pay you?

1005
01:16:07.358 --> 01:16:08.567
I have work.

1006
01:16:08.567 --> 01:16:09.776
I don't know.

1007
01:16:09.776 --> 01:16:12.194
You see what I'm talking about?

1008
01:16:12.194 --> 01:16:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

1009
01:16:13.000 --> 01:16:17.348
<v VINCENT>And then they wonder why people get so damn mad.

1010
01:16:17.348 --> 01:16:22.130
And that's why that guy did what he did yesterday morning.

1011
01:16:22.130 --> 01:16:23.000
In Virginia.

1012
01:16:23.000 --> 01:16:26.478
You saw, I know you say that news.

1013
01:16:26.478 --> 01:16:28.652
You didn't see that news?

1014
01:16:28.652 --> 01:16:29.957
<v ARSENEAULT>Wait what happened?

1015
01:16:29.957 --> 01:16:32.130
<v VINCENT>They killed two news reporters!

1016
01:16:32.130 --> 01:16:34.304
Y'all didn't see that news?

1017
01:16:34.304 --> 01:16:36.043
<v ARSENEAULT>I didn't see that.

1018
01:16:36.043 --> 01:16:37.783
<v VINCENT>Did you see it?

1019
01:16:37.783 --> 01:16:39.087
<v TANGLAO>Yeah, last night.

1020
01:16:39.087 --> 01:16:39.957
<v VINCENT>Hell, yeah.

1021
01:16:39.957 --> 01:16:40.391
Live.

1022
01:16:40.391 --> 01:16:41.696
They were filming.

1023
01:16:41.696 --> 01:16:43.870
<v ARSENEAULT>I did not see that.

1024
01:16:43.870 --> 01:16:46.043
<v VINCENT>He got behind the photographer.

1025
01:16:46.043 --> 01:16:52.130
I guess you know, the photographer probably thought he was just somebody looking on.

1026
01:16:52.130 --> 01:16:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1027
01:16:53.000 --> 01:16:59.000
<v VINCENT>He used to work at the company that they reporting for and they fired him.

1028
01:16:59.000 --> 01:17:00.200
He was mad.

1029
01:17:00.200 --> 01:17:01.400
Young white girl.

1030
01:17:01.400 --> 01:17:05.000
She was twenty-four and the dude was twenty-seven, right?

1031
01:17:05.000 --> 01:17:05.400
<v TANGLAO>Yeah.

1032
01:17:05.400 --> 01:17:08.600
<v VINCENT>He shot both of them dead yesterday morning.

1033
01:17:08.600 --> 01:17:10.600
<v ARSENEAULT>You want to pause it?

1034
01:17:10.600 --> 01:17:11.400
Are you . . .

1035
01:17:11.400 --> 01:17:14.200
<v VINCENT>Keep on, if you got more questions.

1036
01:17:14.200 --> 01:17:18.600
I'm going to see how my help are doing over here.

1037
01:17:18.600 --> 01:17:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, you’re looking at his painting?

1038
01:17:21.000 --> 01:17:22.200
<v VINCENT>You good, man.

1039
01:17:22.200 --> 01:17:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he do a good job?

1040
01:17:25.000 --> 01:17:25.375
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1041
01:17:25.375 --> 01:17:26.875
<v ARSENEAULT>Are you going to . . .

1042
01:17:26.875 --> 01:17:29.875
<v VINCENT>You can make them a little bit thicker.

1043
01:17:29.875 --> 01:17:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Is he fired?

1044
01:17:31.000 --> 01:17:32.875
You going to fire him?

1045
01:17:32.875 --> 01:17:33.625
<v VINCENT>Yeah right.

1046
01:17:33.625 --> 01:17:34.000
Yeah.

1047
01:17:34.000 --> 01:17:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I know you like to fire people.

1048
01:17:37.000 --> 01:17:38.111
<v VINCENT>[laughs] No.

1049
01:17:38.111 --> 01:17:39.778
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm just kidding.

1050
01:17:39.778 --> 01:17:43.111
<v VINCENT>But yeah, that happened yesterday morning.

1051
01:17:43.111 --> 01:17:45.889
I was amazed with that.

1052
01:17:45.889 --> 01:17:46.444
But . . .

1053
01:17:46.444 --> 01:17:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember anything about . . . or people talking about voter

1054
01:17:52.000 --> 01:17:53.667
<c.p>registration in Mossville?

1055
01:17:53.667 --> 01:17:59.778
<v VINCENT>That's another thing we were teaching them, too about that voter.

1056
01:17:59.778 --> 01:18:00.889
In Mossville?

1057
01:18:00.889 --> 01:18:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1058
01:18:02.000 --> 01:18:04.381
<v VINCENT>What about the voter registration?

1059
01:18:04.381 --> 01:18:07.238
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know anything about it?

1060
01:18:07.238 --> 01:18:10.095
If people drove somebody to vote?

1061
01:18:10.095 --> 01:18:12.952
Do they get people involved here?

1062
01:18:12.952 --> 01:18:18.190
<v VINCENT>I don't know because I don't mess with that voting thing.

1063
01:18:18.190 --> 01:18:26.286
But I think they got people that they sent to get people and take them to vote.

1064
01:18:26.286 --> 01:18:29.619
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone . . . Has anyone talked about it?

1065
01:18:29.619 --> 01:18:34.857
Like any of the people who grew up in that time?

1066
01:18:34.857 --> 01:18:38.190
<v VINCENT>Well you mean back in the day?

1067
01:18:38.190 --> 01:18:40.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, like in the sixties.

1068
01:18:40.571 --> 01:18:42.476
<v VINCENT>No. Not about here.

1069
01:18:42.476 --> 01:18:42.952
Washington.

1070
01:18:42.952 --> 01:18:44.381
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, here, too.

1071
01:18:44.381 --> 01:18:50.571
Like did any community leaders here get the community involved in voter registration?

1072
01:18:50.571 --> 01:18:53.429
<v VINCENT>I don't know about back then.

1073
01:18:53.429 --> 01:18:55.810
I think they do now.

1074
01:18:55.810 --> 01:18:58.667
But I don't know about then.

1075
01:18:58.667 --> 01:19:02.000
So what other questions you have?

1076
01:19:02.000 --> 01:19:02.616
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright.

1077
01:19:02.616 --> 01:19:08.164
I was going to ask you about the protests?

1078
01:19:08.164 --> 01:19:12.479
Your memories of any of the protests.

1079
01:19:12.479 --> 01:19:18.027
<v VINCENT>So much been going on in those protests, man.

1080
01:19:18.027 --> 01:19:21.726
You ever been to a protest?

1081
01:19:21.726 --> 01:19:24.192
<v ARSENEAULT>No. What's it like?

1082
01:19:24.192 --> 01:19:30.973
<v VINCENT>Well one thing, all the protestors are on the same page.

1083
01:19:30.973 --> 01:19:41.452
They all get together and raising hell about whatever the issue is at that time, you know.

1084
01:19:41.452 --> 01:19:51.932
And as usual they got plenty of cops around to try to lock up everybody they can.

1085
01:19:51.932 --> 01:19:56.247
Break everybody's spirits they can, you know.

1086
01:19:56.247 --> 01:19:59.945
A lot of passing out flyers.

1087
01:19:59.945 --> 01:20:03.027
A lot of people networking.

1088
01:20:03.027 --> 01:20:17.205
Getting together even more and making plans where they going to get together, what they going to do, who's going to be involved.

1089
01:20:17.205 --> 01:20:19.671
Lot of organizing, basically.

1090
01:20:19.671 --> 01:20:30.767
<v ARSENEAULT>So it sounds like it's a chance for people who have the same ideals to kind of organize?

1091
01:20:30.767 --> 01:20:32.000
<v VINCENT>Yes.

1092
01:20:32.000 --> 01:20:35.943
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any protests in particular?

1093
01:20:35.943 --> 01:20:38.195
<v VINCENT>Any one in particular?

1094
01:20:38.195 --> 01:20:45.517
That one that Martin Luther King was at was, I remember that particular.

1095
01:20:45.517 --> 01:20:55.655
For one reason that I was right up front and it was a whole lot of people there.

1096
01:20:55.655 --> 01:21:10.862
They were all the way from the monument to the . . . I can't even think of the name of that where the boy's sitting up in the chair.

1097
01:21:10.862 --> 01:21:11.989
<v ARSENEAULT>Lincoln Memorial?

1098
01:21:11.989 --> 01:21:12.552
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1099
01:21:12.552 --> 01:21:14.241
Lincoln Memorial, yeah.

1100
01:21:14.241 --> 01:21:24.379
That was the main one I remember, but a lot of them broke out in disturbances and whatnot.

1101
01:21:24.379 --> 01:21:31.701
Had them shooting tear gas at us and stuff like that, you know.

1102
01:21:31.701 --> 01:21:42.402
Because I went to protest in a lot of different states, too, like New York, Philly [Philadelphia] and Virginia.

1103
01:21:42.402 --> 01:21:55.920
We went to Virginia one time because they were messing with these brothers over there and did you see that truck that I had?

1104
01:21:55.920 --> 01:21:58.736
The truck in the picture?

1105
01:21:58.736 --> 01:22:00.425
We drove that.

1106
01:22:00.425 --> 01:22:05.494
That's the master truck that we used to drive.

1107
01:22:05.494 --> 01:22:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, was it a Panther Party truck?

1108
01:22:10.000 --> 01:22:10.755
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1109
01:22:10.755 --> 01:22:17.551
It's in the background on one of those pictures.

1110
01:22:17.551 --> 01:22:21.327
Looked like a paddy wagon.

1111
01:22:21.327 --> 01:22:25.102
<v ARSENEAULT>Y'all drove a paddy wagon?

1112
01:22:25.102 --> 01:22:40.204
<v VINCENT>Yeah we drove that to Virginia because they were having trouble over there and . . . Must be this one right here.

1113
01:22:40.204 --> 01:22:42.469
<v ARSENEAULT>It's this one?

1114
01:22:42.469 --> 01:22:44.735
<v VINCENT>There it is.

1115
01:22:44.735 --> 01:22:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh wow.

1116
01:22:47.000 --> 01:22:48.944
<v VINCENT>That's up on Evergreen as we speak.

1117
01:22:48.944 --> 01:22:49.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Evergreen Road?

1118
01:22:49.500 --> 01:22:51.444
<v VINCENT>It’s yellow now, yeah, but it had . . .

1119
01:22:51.444 --> 01:22:52.278
<v ARSENEAULT>It's still there?

1120
01:22:52.278 --> 01:22:54.222
<v VINCENT>. . . Malcolm X on the side of it.

1121
01:22:54.222 --> 01:22:55.611
You can't really see it.

1122
01:22:55.611 --> 01:22:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that your motorcycle?

1123
01:22:57.000 --> 01:22:57.417
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1124
01:22:57.417 --> 01:23:04.500
It had a Malcolm X on the side and it had loudspeakers on each side of it.

1125
01:23:04.500 --> 01:23:07.417
<v ARSENEAULT>What would you say on the speakers?

1126
01:23:07.417 --> 01:23:10.333
<v VINCENT>Telling people, you know, about what's happening.

1127
01:23:10.333 --> 01:23:11.583
What to do.

1128
01:23:11.583 --> 01:23:13.250
How to stand up.

1129
01:23:13.250 --> 01:23:14.083
You know.

1130
01:23:14.083 --> 01:23:16.583
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever get pulled over?

1131
01:23:16.583 --> 01:23:19.083
<v VINCENT>No, we'd be in the community.

1132
01:23:19.083 --> 01:23:21.167
We never got pulled over.

1133
01:23:21.167 --> 01:23:24.083
But we had seats in the back.

1134
01:23:24.083 --> 01:23:28.250
We had maybe about eight or ten people back there.

1135
01:23:28.250 --> 01:23:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So this is on Evergreen Road right now?

1136
01:23:32.000 --> 01:23:32.541
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1137
01:23:32.541 --> 01:23:34.703
It's painted yellow now.

1138
01:23:34.703 --> 01:23:39.568
It's for sale, if you'd like to have it.

1139
01:23:39.568 --> 01:23:40.649
<v ARSENEAULT>I would.

1140
01:23:40.649 --> 01:23:41.189
<v VINCENT>[Laughs]

1141
01:23:41.189 --> 01:23:43.351
<v ARSENEAULT>I would like it.

1142
01:23:43.351 --> 01:23:50.378
<v VINCENT>Yeah, so we went over to Virginia to support the people over there.

1143
01:23:50.378 --> 01:23:56.865
They locked me up over there, because I was the so-called ringleader.

1144
01:23:56.865 --> 01:24:04.432
But they didn't hold me long because people started gathering around the police station.

1145
01:24:04.432 --> 01:24:08.216
<v ARSENEAULT>So they locked you up in Virginia?

1146
01:24:08.216 --> 01:24:08.757
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1147
01:24:08.757 --> 01:24:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you see it coming?

1148
01:24:12.000 --> 01:24:13.113
<v VINCENT>Not really.

1149
01:24:13.113 --> 01:24:17.009
They charged me with being, you know . . .

1150
01:24:17.009 --> 01:24:17.565
<v ARSENEAULT>Instigator.

1151
01:24:17.565 --> 01:24:18.678
<v VINCENT>Instigator, right.

1152
01:24:18.678 --> 01:24:20.348
That's the word.

1153
01:24:20.348 --> 01:24:26.470
They don't want us to come together and support each other.

1154
01:24:26.470 --> 01:24:30.365
Because they know there's strength in numbers.

1155
01:24:30.365 --> 01:24:34.817
But they just, they didn't hold me long.

1156
01:24:34.817 --> 01:24:45.948
They just locked me up for maybe about a couple of hours or something like that. [ . . .?] I guess they said,

1157
01:24:45.948 --> 01:24:49.287
"Well we better let him out."

1158
01:24:49.287 --> 01:24:54.852
And they instructed us, “Y’all better get out of town.

1159
01:24:54.852 --> 01:24:58.191
We don’t want you all around.”

1160
01:24:58.191 --> 01:25:01.530
<v ARSENEAULT>So this is on Evergreen Road.

1161
01:25:01.530 --> 01:25:07.096
Have you heard any stories about Evergreen Road being haunted?

1162
01:25:07.096 --> 01:25:12.661
<v VINCENT>Yeah, they used to tell . . . Now that you mention that,

1163
01:25:12.661 --> 01:25:27.130
they used to tell me . . . My uncle used to tell me that there was a light that you could see off in the distance at night.

1164
01:25:27.130 --> 01:25:36.035
And you walked towards that light, you’d never get any closer to it. [Laughs] You know?

1165
01:25:36.035 --> 01:25:37.704
Stories like that.

1166
01:25:37.704 --> 01:25:42.713
And you know, here, then there wasn't no lights.

1167
01:25:42.713 --> 01:25:44.939
No streetlights and stuff.

1168
01:25:44.939 --> 01:25:49.391
So that was . . . That sounded weird to me.

1169
01:25:49.391 --> 01:25:52.174
What's happening with that light?

1170
01:25:52.174 --> 01:25:54.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Any other ghost stories?

1171
01:25:54.400 --> 01:25:56.070
<v VINCENT>Let me see.

1172
01:25:56.070 --> 01:25:59.965
That was the main one I remember.

1173
01:25:59.965 --> 01:26:02.748
I don't remember any others.

1174
01:26:02.748 --> 01:26:06.643
Seem like maybe . . . I'm trying to remember.

1175
01:26:06.643 --> 01:26:11.652
That's the major one I remember at this time.

1176
01:26:11.652 --> 01:26:20.000
I don't know if they had a name for that light or not but . . .

1177
01:26:20.000 --> 01:26:23.375
<v ARSENEAULT>We've heard some stories about Evergreen Road being haunted.

1178
01:26:23.375 --> 01:26:24.875
<v VINCENT>What stories you heard?

1179
01:26:24.875 --> 01:26:28.250
<v ARSENEAULT>There's a man with no face who'll show up.

1180
01:26:28.250 --> 01:26:32.375
And a haunted sycamore tree at the end of Evergreen Road.

1181
01:26:32.375 --> 01:26:33.125
<v VINCENT>A haunted . . .?

1182
01:26:33.125 --> 01:26:34.625
<v ARSENEAULT>A haunted sycamore tree.

1183
01:26:34.625 --> 01:26:35.375
<v VINCENT>Oh yeah.

1184
01:26:35.375 --> 01:26:38.375
<v ARSENEAULT>Or a cypress tree, I don't remember which.

1185
01:26:38.375 --> 01:26:41.000
But the tree itself is haunted.

1186
01:26:41.000 --> 01:26:42.074
<v VINCENT>Oh yeah.

1187
01:26:42.074 --> 01:26:51.204
So what kind of action would happen around the tree for it to be haunted, you know?

1188
01:26:51.204 --> 01:26:52.815
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't know.

1189
01:26:52.815 --> 01:26:53.352
<v VINCENT>Okay.

1190
01:26:53.352 --> 01:26:59.796
<v ARSENEAULT>But kids were scared to go trick or treating on Evergreen Road.

1191
01:26:59.796 --> 01:27:04.093
<v VINCENT>Yeah, I don't know much about haunted stuff.

1192
01:27:04.093 --> 01:27:07.315
<v ARSENEAULT>You'll have to ask Ms. Julia.

1193
01:27:07.315 --> 01:27:08.926
She'll tell you.

1194
01:27:08.926 --> 01:27:10.000
<v VINCENT>Okay.

1195
01:27:10.000 --> 01:27:15.583
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know anything about any healers in the area?

1196
01:27:15.583 --> 01:27:21.167
Was there ever anybody who was a healer in Mossville?

1197
01:27:21.167 --> 01:27:23.958
<v VINCENT>Oh yeah, let me see.

1198
01:27:23.958 --> 01:27:26.192
What was his name?

1199
01:27:26.192 --> 01:27:29.542
I think his name was Mamou.

1200
01:27:29.542 --> 01:27:30.658
He was

1201
01:27:30.658 --> 01:27:31.775
<c.p>a healer.

1202
01:27:31.775 --> 01:27:35.125
I met him once or twice.

1203
01:27:35.125 --> 01:27:39.592
But he used to . . . He'd pick up herbs.

1204
01:27:39.592 --> 01:27:47.967
He'd know different herbs that grow around and he used to heal people with that.

1205
01:27:47.967 --> 01:27:50.200
Can't remember the herb.

1206
01:27:50.200 --> 01:27:54.108
But my significant other is a healer.

1207
01:27:54.108 --> 01:27:59.133
Pull off the tip of that and taste it.

1208
01:27:59.133 --> 01:28:00.808
That's an herb.

1209
01:28:00.808 --> 01:28:02.483
<v ARSENEAULT>Just eat it?

1210
01:28:02.483 --> 01:28:05.833
<v VINCENT>Tell me what it tastes like?

1211
01:28:05.833 --> 01:28:07.508
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a pickle.

1212
01:28:07.508 --> 01:28:08.625
That's dill.

1213
01:28:08.625 --> 01:28:11.417
<v VINCENT>You want to taste this?

1214
01:28:11.417 --> 01:28:17.000
Pull the top of it off and taste it.

1215
01:28:17.000 --> 01:28:19.041
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that what that is?

1216
01:28:19.041 --> 01:28:26.388
<v VINCENT>I don't know if it's dill or not, but it's got a nice little taste to it though.

1217
01:28:26.388 --> 01:28:28.020
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a pickle, huh?

1218
01:28:28.020 --> 01:28:30.061
<v VINCENT>Yeah, but it's an herb.

1219
01:28:30.061 --> 01:28:30.469
<v ARSENEAULT>Fresh.

1220
01:28:30.469 --> 01:28:31.286
It's good.

1221
01:28:31.286 --> 01:28:32.918
<v VINCENT>What it taste like?

1222
01:28:32.918 --> 01:28:34.143
<v TANGLAO>Almost like mint.

1223
01:28:34.143 --> 01:28:34.551
Almost.

1224
01:28:34.551 --> 01:28:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It is kind of minty.

1225
01:28:37.000 --> 01:28:37.906
<v TANGLAO>Like minty.

1226
01:28:37.906 --> 01:28:38.811
<v VINCENT>Kind of.

1227
01:28:38.811 --> 01:28:41.528
That’s one right there beside you.

1228
01:28:41.528 --> 01:28:43.792
They grow real tall, too.

1229
01:28:43.792 --> 01:28:46.509
<v ARSENEAULT>What does she do with it?

1230
01:28:46.509 --> 01:28:47.868
<v VINCENT>I don't know.

1231
01:28:47.868 --> 01:28:52.396
It's got a nice little mellow taste to it, though.

1232
01:28:52.396 --> 01:28:53.302
<v ARSENEAULT>It's nice.

1233
01:28:53.302 --> 01:28:56.019
<v VINCENT>Maybe y'all can check it out.

1234
01:28:56.019 --> 01:28:59.189
I haven't really did research on it.

1235
01:28:59.189 --> 01:29:01.906
But it grow wild around here.

1236
01:29:01.906 --> 01:29:06.887
Just like [Saphira?] she knew a lot about herbs and whatnot.

1237
01:29:06.887 --> 01:29:10.962
We got some stuff going around here called callaloo.

1238
01:29:10.962 --> 01:29:13.679
It's like first cousins to spinach.

1239
01:29:13.679 --> 01:29:19.566
Matter of fact, that's what she's using on me now for my prostate.

1240
01:29:19.566 --> 01:29:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She making something out of it or you just eating it?

1241
01:29:25.000 --> 01:29:27.337
<v VINCENT>We eating it raw.

1242
01:29:27.337 --> 01:29:32.011
She fixed a drink for me this morning.

1243
01:29:32.011 --> 01:29:33.180
Green drink.

1244
01:29:33.180 --> 01:29:35.517
<v ARSENEAULT>She blend it up?

1245
01:29:35.517 --> 01:29:36.101
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1246
01:29:36.101 --> 01:29:37.854
She's pretty good.

1247
01:29:37.854 --> 01:29:39.022
She's Indian.

1248
01:29:39.022 --> 01:29:44.865
So she knows quite a bit about herbs and stuff.

1249
01:29:44.865 --> 01:29:47.787
<v ARSENEAULT>Is she from the area?

1250
01:29:47.787 --> 01:29:50.708
<v VINCENT>No. She's from another state.

1251
01:29:50.708 --> 01:29:53.629
But she live here now.

1252
01:29:53.629 --> 01:29:57.719
Matter of fact, what time is it?

1253
01:29:57.719 --> 01:29:59.472
<v ARSENEAULT>Time to go?

1254
01:29:59.472 --> 01:30:02.978
<v VINCENT>No, she should be coming up.

1255
01:30:02.978 --> 01:30:04.146
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

1256
01:30:04.146 --> 01:30:05.315
<v VINCENT>Pretty soon.

1257
01:30:05.315 --> 01:30:09.404
<v ARSENEAULT>Well I can start wrapping it up.

1258
01:30:09.404 --> 01:30:11.157
Some final questions.

1259
01:30:11.157 --> 01:30:11.742
<v VINCENT>Alright.

1260
01:30:11.742 --> 01:30:12.326
True.

1261
01:30:12.326 --> 01:30:17.000
You saved me a little time now.

1262
01:30:17.000 --> 01:30:20.094
<v ARSENEAULT>So your fondest memory of Mossville?

1263
01:30:20.094 --> 01:30:23.705
<v VINCENT>Fondest memory is when I moved here.

1264
01:30:23.705 --> 01:30:27.831
No, when I was about to move here.

1265
01:30:27.831 --> 01:30:31.957
After I got the check from the lawyer.

1266
01:30:31.957 --> 01:30:35.051
No, before I got the check.

1267
01:30:35.051 --> 01:30:42.272
My honey at that time, the one that picked me up from the penitentiary.

1268
01:30:42.272 --> 01:30:53.618
I borrowed some money from her and her car to come down here to see, you know, what I was coming to.

1269
01:30:53.618 --> 01:30:57.744
Because I hadn't been there in a while.

1270
01:30:57.744 --> 01:31:07.028
I came down, let me see, must have been in the seventies after I got out, I believe.

1271
01:31:07.028 --> 01:31:14.248
I was bringing a friend . . . I was going to Arizona to see my children.

1272
01:31:14.248 --> 01:31:21.984
And this friend of my aunt was a Mexican brother who worked with my aunt.

1273
01:31:21.984 --> 01:31:25.594
And he was going back to Mexico.

1274
01:31:25.594 --> 01:31:35.394
So she introduced us and he wanted to ride with me, you know, because he was going back home.

1275
01:31:35.394 --> 01:31:45.193
So I had a Volkswagen at the time and we drove that and I went to Mexico with him.

1276
01:31:45.193 --> 01:31:50.350
I went to Juarez, Mexico and stayed at his place.

1277
01:31:50.350 --> 01:31:54.476
And then I went to visit my children.

1278
01:31:54.476 --> 01:31:59.118
And let me see, I think I'm getting off.

1279
01:31:59.118 --> 01:32:07.370
But anyway, I borrowed some money from her and borrowed her car and we came here.

1280
01:32:07.370 --> 01:32:09.433
Me and my brother.

1281
01:32:09.433 --> 01:32:19.232
And when I went on that piece of land up there that they stole, there's a big old tree.

1282
01:32:19.232 --> 01:32:25.421
Have you seen that tree in Lake Charles at the historical museum?

1283
01:32:25.421 --> 01:32:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't think so.

1284
01:32:28.000 --> 01:32:31.262
<v VINCENT>But anyway, it's a big old tree.

1285
01:32:31.262 --> 01:32:34.058
Limbs come down to the ground.

1286
01:32:34.058 --> 01:32:35.922
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a live oak?

1287
01:32:35.922 --> 01:32:38.718
<v VINCENT>You need to see that tree.

1288
01:32:38.718 --> 01:32:39.184
Yeah.

1289
01:32:39.184 --> 01:32:39.650
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright.

1290
01:32:39.650 --> 01:32:40.583
<v VINCENT>Live oak.

1291
01:32:40.583 --> 01:32:41.981
Old live oak.

1292
01:32:41.981 --> 01:32:44.777
But our tree was like that.

1293
01:32:44.777 --> 01:32:54.563
You could step like you stepping up on some steps or something and walk all the way up in the tree.

1294
01:32:54.563 --> 01:33:05.748
But when I came down here me and my brother went on the old land and when we went up and saw that tree,

1295
01:33:05.748 --> 01:33:12.738
man, I could feel the vibrations of this is where I was supposed to be.

1296
01:33:12.738 --> 01:33:17.398
Because all of the history was coming back, you know.

1297
01:33:17.398 --> 01:33:24.854
All the memories that I had from my daddy and my uncle telling me about it.

1298
01:33:24.854 --> 01:33:26.718
It was really deep.

1299
01:33:26.718 --> 01:33:29.049
It was like really inspiring.

1300
01:33:29.049 --> 01:33:32.311
That was one of my main memories.

1301
01:33:32.311 --> 01:33:40.233
Was all that history I was told was coming back to me when I came down here.

1302
01:33:40.233 --> 01:33:42.097
It was really inspiring.

1303
01:33:42.097 --> 01:33:48.155
So I went back and decided on packing up and going back home.

1304
01:33:48.155 --> 01:33:56.078
So I packed up all my stuff in a station wagon and I was pulling the Volkswagen.

1305
01:33:56.078 --> 01:34:03.068
We went to Wisconsin and my sister's husband gave me a jeep and a tractor.

1306
01:34:03.068 --> 01:34:07.728
That old tractor that's down in the field down there.

1307
01:34:07.728 --> 01:34:14.252
I don't know if y'all saw it when you was coming up the street.

1308
01:34:14.252 --> 01:34:19.379
This old farm tractor, he gave me that and a jeep.

1309
01:34:19.379 --> 01:34:22.175
And I bought a big truck.

1310
01:34:22.175 --> 01:34:26.835
Put the tractor in the truck and towed the jeep.

1311
01:34:26.835 --> 01:34:34.291
So I had a little caravan coming down here. [Laughs] Came down here and this house,

1312
01:34:34.291 --> 01:34:40.816
that little house over there, was, the lights were on, the water was on.

1313
01:34:40.816 --> 01:34:43.612
I said, "This is a blessing."

1314
01:34:43.612 --> 01:34:44.544
You know?

1315
01:34:44.544 --> 01:34:48.738
But that was one of my most fondest memories.

1316
01:34:48.738 --> 01:34:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It was ready to move in?

1317
01:34:52.000 --> 01:34:54.800
<v VINCENT>It was all ready to move in.

1318
01:34:54.800 --> 01:34:58.000
But what’s happening, my uncle had passed away.

1319
01:34:58.000 --> 01:35:01.200
And his girlfriend was trying to homestead it.

1320
01:35:01.200 --> 01:35:04.400
You know, she was trying to get it.

1321
01:35:04.400 --> 01:35:08.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Trying to live there for so long to where it . . .

1322
01:35:08.400 --> 01:35:08.800
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1323
01:35:08.800 --> 01:35:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>…automatically became hers.

1324
01:35:10.000 --> 01:35:10.400
<v VINCENT>Right.

1325
01:35:10.400 --> 01:35:14.000
So I blocked that when I moved in.

1326
01:35:14.000 --> 01:35:16.795
<v ARSENEAULT>How did she react to that?

1327
01:35:16.795 --> 01:35:19.590
<v VINCENT>Oh, she was fighting against it.

1328
01:35:19.590 --> 01:35:26.578
And she had a man in the water company that she was messing around with.

1329
01:35:26.578 --> 01:35:29.839
She had the water in her name.

1330
01:35:29.839 --> 01:35:34.498
I think she had the lights in her name, too.

1331
01:35:34.498 --> 01:35:42.418
But I changed the lights and he didn't want to change the water out of her name.

1332
01:35:42.418 --> 01:35:46.610
So we got into a little altercation about that.

1333
01:35:46.610 --> 01:35:47.076
But . . .

1334
01:35:47.076 --> 01:35:48.474
<v ARSENEAULT>It got resolved?

1335
01:35:48.474 --> 01:35:48.940
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1336
01:35:48.940 --> 01:35:53.598
So she didn't achieve what she was trying to achieve.

1337
01:35:53.598 --> 01:35:57.791
So she was having the road and everything [unclear].

1338
01:35:57.791 --> 01:36:04.313
And they got people coming in offering me money for this piece of land.

1339
01:36:04.313 --> 01:36:06.643
But they ain't offering enough.

1340
01:36:06.643 --> 01:36:12.233
Because as you see, you put a business right here, right now?

1341
01:36:12.233 --> 01:36:14.562
You go it going on.

1342
01:36:14.562 --> 01:36:16.892
Money passing by every day.

1343
01:36:16.892 --> 01:36:20.618
So that's why I'm putting this out there.

1344
01:36:20.618 --> 01:36:24.345
Y'all come and get some fresh vegetables. [Laughs]

1345
01:36:24.345 --> 01:36:25.743
<v ARSENEAULT>Make some money.

1346
01:36:25.743 --> 01:36:26.209
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

1347
01:36:26.209 --> 01:36:29.004
But I'm willing to lease it.

1348
01:36:29.004 --> 01:36:40.651
But see Sasol, they, I don't know what they up to, I’m sure they trying to, same thing that the government did with this piece.

1349
01:36:40.651 --> 01:36:42.980
What do they call it?

1350
01:36:42.980 --> 01:36:43.912
Eminent domain.

1351
01:36:43.912 --> 01:36:47.173
So they trying to improve the area.

1352
01:36:47.173 --> 01:36:52.297
So they try to get me to give them this land.

1353
01:36:52.297 --> 01:37:02.546
I said, "Do I look like I'm in a position to give you some land? [Laughs] Do I look crazy or what?”

1354
01:37:02.546 --> 01:37:03.012
So . . .

1355
01:37:03.012 --> 01:37:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm curious how when you moved here . . . How did the community react to you?

1356
01:37:10.000 --> 01:37:21.716
<v VINCENT>I told you how the youngsters reacted because I stopped . . . Well they didn't accept me because I'm a Yankee from the east.

1357
01:37:21.716 --> 01:37:22.781
You know?

1358
01:37:22.781 --> 01:37:28.107
We all black, but I'm a Yankee from the east.

1359
01:37:28.107 --> 01:37:38.757
So they never really did accept me because I wasn't raised with them and I'm different from them, you know.

1360
01:37:38.757 --> 01:37:39.290
So . . .

1361
01:37:39.290 --> 01:37:44.083
<v ARSENEAULT>What did that feel like to be an outsider?

1362
01:37:44.083 --> 01:37:48.343
<v VINCENT>It didn't feel good, but I didn't care.

1363
01:37:48.343 --> 01:37:54.201
Because I know that I'm part of the roots, you know.

1364
01:37:54.201 --> 01:37:57.929
Whether they knew or cared or not.

1365
01:37:57.929 --> 01:38:06.450
It didn't make no difference to me because I'm working on something that belong to us.

1366
01:38:06.450 --> 01:38:12.308
In other words, it's handing on and I'm doing my part.

1367
01:38:12.308 --> 01:38:23.491
Since it's handed to me now, I'm doing my part to pass it on to my daughters and them, you know.

1368
01:38:23.491 --> 01:38:27.219
Because the paperwork on it is patented.

1369
01:38:27.219 --> 01:38:30.414
Did I tell you about patent?

1370
01:38:30.414 --> 01:38:36.272
Patent is when . . . There you go, you can see a patent.

1371
01:38:36.272 --> 01:38:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So that's the original document or . . .?

1372
01:38:40.000 --> 01:38:40.570
<v VINCENT>Yes.

1373
01:38:40.570 --> 01:38:41.709
This is . . .

1374
01:38:41.709 --> 01:38:42.278
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1375
01:38:42.278 --> 01:38:42.848
Okay.

1376
01:38:42.848 --> 01:38:43.987
<v VINCENT>Land patents.

1377
01:38:43.987 --> 01:38:47.974
<v TANGLAO>You want me to take a picture?

1378
01:38:47.974 --> 01:38:48.543
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure.

1379
01:38:48.543 --> 01:38:49.113
What . . .

1380
01:38:49.113 --> 01:38:51.391
<v VINCENT>Read it to me.

1381
01:38:51.391 --> 01:38:53.669
I can't read. [Laughs]

1382
01:38:53.669 --> 01:38:56.517
<v ARSENEAULT>I can hardly see this.

1383
